{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '59,79p;221,274p' AGENTS.md; rg -n '032|H4|outside Data Center' docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PILOT-RESULT.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '59,79p;221,274p' AGENTS.md; rg -n '032|H4|outside Data Center' docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PILOT-RESULT.md\"","aggregated_output":"## 1. Before acting on a failure: diagnose\n\nA failure is a question, not a task. Nothing changes (code, prompt, configuration, test, gold) until these are written:\n\n1. **The class.** Count every instance on stored data, at no cost: how many, since when, what each costs; never only the case in view.\n   Match by path or field, not by substring, and check the census on one case whose answer is known. A flagged word in a document is a\n   class too: count it over the whole scope before the first edit.\n2. **The mechanism.** Trace one case end to end (the raw output, every check that dropped it, the input that produced it, the code\n   that built that input) to the design choice that produces the whole class. First check whether your own last change or load caused\n   it; when several mechanisms stack on the same cases, find them all first.\n3. **Step back.** Is it the product's problem, or one the measurement or process made? Read the governing standard and the decisions on\n   the topic before writing any rule: the answer is often written already, and a question about the measuring stick is answered by the\n   frozen ruler (§7.1), never by a new ruler (Alex, 2026-10-29: \"我们是聊过这个事情的你难道没看文档吗\").\n4. **The cheapest proof.** Show the change flips the decisive case on real data (a stored input edited and re-run, a rule run on a copy\n   of stored data) and count what else it fires or touches. If it does not flip, the cause is elsewhere: trace again and build nothing.\n5. **The share.** Show what share of the counted class the fix removes. A fix that removes part of it is a patch: say so, and do not\n   build it as the fix. A fix that another change subsumes is not built.\n\nBefore any design: ten raw answers read beside their inputs, the whole request read as the model reads it, and, for a choice the model\nmade, the model's own account (continue the stored conversation with \"which instruction led you here, and which two statements did you\nreject\", a cent a case).\n  contradiction, rule written for another case, instruction that forces the defect, reference the model cannot read, payload field the\n  answer does not need, and call doing several jobs. Cut, merge or split. Add an instruction only when the audit shows one is missing,\n  and remove at least as much elsewhere. Report characters and prompt tokens before and after; growth needs a written reason.\n- **Trace a miss to the raw answer first.** Class it as not written, refused (by which check) or split. Check that the request shows what\n  the answer needs and the schema can express it before changing any wording.\n- **An example is an instruction.** It must agree with the standard, the schema and the code that reads the answer, or it goes.\n- **A classification the model must make is written as a closed set with examples:** two or three examples per class, the procedure\n  (walk the whole page, first paragraph to last) and the stopping point (\"a page with eight such statements yields eight evidence\n  items\"). An abstract clause (\"a material statement\") is a coin flip per send. The examples are not growth to avoid; the abstract clause\n  they replace is cut.\n- **Check before the send, at no cost.** Write one answer that follows the prompt and run every code check on it: a request never asks\n  for what a check refuses, and a retry that repairs a refusal is a symptom, not handling. Read every contract line against each gold\n  case it must accept and reject. Rebuild a stored request from the stored request, never from a ledger that moves, and check every\n  identity before a paid send.\n- **Build where code decides.** A decision code can make is moved out of the model, not sampled (identity only, §2).\n\n## 7. Measuring and iterating\n\n### 7.1 The ruler\n\n- **One ruler.** Every gold, judge, prompt and acceptance uses the one standard named before the first arm. A second ruler in use (an old\n  reference, a judge's rubric, a prompt's own categories) is a defect, fixed before anything is measured (D241, Alex: \"我们今天一直浪费时间在不同标准之间来回\").\n- **The frozen ruler (D261, D262; 2026-11-02)** remains the standard for existing measurements and historical comparisons until a separately reviewed replacement is established. For future evidence scoring (D372), combine the existing gold's core and supporting as helpful, treat its not-kept entries as unhelpful, and leave optional entries neutral; this does not alter the frozen files. Its pieces, with the hashes code checks:\n  1. The extraction standard: `docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html`, its version 5 (version 3 as signed in D243, with D248's seven\n     boundaries and D250's four evidence boundaries and counting unit); the gold `docs/research/2026-10-29-ledger-standard/golden-v5/` (dev sha256 `6f9476bb…`, test `af2145cd…`, recorded\n     in `golden-v5.sha256`). There is no version 6.\n  2. The questions: golden-v5's must_open / may_open (questions standard version 3) and the seeds `poc/seeds/nvda-2026-07-01-v2.json`\n     (`fca16f42…`, 22 questions). The seeds are part of the ruler: the judgement scorer refuses a run whose seed questions differ; a new\n     seed set is a new ruler version, opened only by Alex's decision.\n  3. The judgement ruler (D3): `docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/`: `ruler-v1.md` (the definition), `reader-brief.md`,\n     `packet-v1.json` (golden-v5's 309 items, `53d1e8b4…`), `questions-v1.json` (the 22 seeds plus the Ohio guarantee, `30854180…`),\n     `gold-v1.json` = reader B's answers (`636c056d…`, in `gold-v1.sha256`), `score_judgement.py` (refuses any other sha256 or ruler\n     version), `corrections-v1.json` (dated records only, printed with every score, never merged).\n\n  None of these files changes again (`tests/test_frozen_rulers.py` fails the suite when a hash moves; Alex, 2026-11-02: \"你的尺子做出来和金标真的不能再动了，\n  已经造成了3天的各种漂移，非常的严重 … 我们做完的review过的尺子金标不能再动了\"). A defect found later is a dated correction record beside the gold,\n  with its reason and its before and after, printed with every score and never merged in. Every measurement names the gold version it used. A new piece (the D2\n  matter-to-question mapping, not yet built, is one) is an addition recorded as such, never an edit of these. A new version of any piece\n  exists only by Alex's decision, derives its gold from scratch, and old scores are never compared with it. A disagreement between the\n  system and the gold is a gap in the system to close (D263), never a case for the ruler. A suspected inconsistency between gold and\n  standard no longer stops an arm: record the concern separately, continue scoring on the unchanged frozen gold, and disclose it with the results (D373).\n  What it measures: extraction against the gold's items, questions against must_open / may_open, the judgement at the window end\n  2026-07-14 against reader B's ranges (a numeric question agrees when the gold range overlaps the chosen rung's band, a yes/no question\n  when the answers match). What it does not measure: the judgement's daily path, and whether evidence was filed under the right question.\n- **The extraction standard** (piece 1) is the extraction step's PRD, signed by Alex on 2026-10-29 (D243: \"其他几条的意见我都ok，sign off\"),\n  which replaced the first version (D229: \"按照这套标准来开始做测量，这条标准不要漂移\"). It holds the ledger's three records, the five question\n  types, the opening tests, the evidence rule and its reject list, source tiers, weight, and how a prompt change is written, measured and\n  iterated. No arm, gold, judge or prompt adds a rule, an example or a weight the page does not hold; a change is a recorded decision in\n  Alex's words first, then a new version of the page, then a drift review.\n- **A ruler or gold that is built and reviewed is never changed again** (D261). Reviewed means readers who did not write it have read it\n  against the decisions and Alex has signed it. A ruler under construction is edited only until that review, and nothing is scored\n  against it before.\n- **The ruler is Alex's, never your own.** Gold, unit and the judge's reading are checked against what the product uses and fixed with\n  Alex before the first arm. A standard or reference you write gives every sentence its source (the signed standard, a decision, a PRD)\n24:| H4: CPU double count in total revenue | **4 answers** (07-15, 07-16, 07-17, 07-18; in force 07-15..07-19) | no CPU link | Claude read all 71 post-link v14 answers; Codex did a shuffled second read with dates hidden (exploratory, because the list was in the brief); both lists agree |\n33:### H4\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-whole-and-part/claude-r43b.md; rg -n '\"'^## D(262|263|711|719|720)'\"\\\\b' docs/DECISIONS-log.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-whole-and-part/claude-r43b.md; rg -n '\"'^## D(262|263|711|719|720)'\"\\\\b' docs/DECISIONS-log.md\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Claude's position before round 43b (written before Codex's answer)\n     2\t\n     3\t**The finding.** Checking D263 against Codex's finding 5, I read the frozen judgement gold\n     4\t(`judgement-gold/gold-v1.json`, reader B, D262).\n     5\t- **q01, total revenue at 07-14, band $400–435B.** The gold builds the second half above consensus's $219B. It lists management's\n     6\t  \"$20bn of Vera CPU sales by fiscal year-end (i288)\" among the drivers, beside growth acceleration, SemiAnalysis's 20% compute uplift\n     7\t  and the Rubin ramp: \"A second half of $228–262bn gives $400–435bn.\"\n     8\t- **q19, stand-alone Vera at 07-14, band $8–18B.** The target is discounted for two selling quarters.\n     9\t- **q02, Data Center:** it applies 92–93% to the q01 total.\n    10\t\n    11\tSo the frozen standard treats a not-yet-shipping product's target as upside above consensus and above the guided run-rate, discounted.\n    12\tIt does not treat the target as already inside the basis. D263 wrote judging-v7 to that reasoning, and a gap to the gold is closed in\n    13\tthe judge. The ruler is Alex's (AGENTS §7.1).\n    14\t\n    15\t**Consequences, as I read them:**\n    16\t1. The premise of DIAGNOSIS.md, \"a basis formed after the company published the target already contains it\", is my own analyst\n    17\t   view. It contradicts the frozen gold. The edit I proposed would move the judge away from the gold.\n    18\t2. old-1's discounted additions follow the gold's reasoning. Its 07-14 state, about $430B, is in the gold band.\n    19\t3. linked-1's four \"double counts\" (about $407B) lie inside the gold band at 07-15..07-18. The answers that did not add (about $387B)\n    20\t   lie below it. H4 (\"double counting the CPU forecast is harm\", PAID-DESIGN.md) was a council harm line written without the gold's\n    21\t   reasoning, and my pilot reading followed it.\n    22\t4. What may still be a defect:\n    23\t   - **(a) Inconsistency.** The same judge flips between \"included\" and \"add\" on adjacent days, because no sentence settles it. The\n    24\t     gold settles it the other way from my proposal.\n    25\t   - **(b) The amount.** linked-1 added v8's $19.5B, while the gold for stand-alone Vera is $8–18B. That is v8 over-reading the target\n    26\t     (the gap D262 named), not v14's arithmetic.\n    27\t   - **(c) Wording.** v9 on 07-15 said CPU is \"outside Data Center\", a wrong statement with no number effect.\n    28\t\n    29\t**My lean:** close the double-count design with nothing to build. The class as framed is not a defect by the frozen ruler. Report this\n    30\tto Alex as a corrected premise, and record that H4 conflicted with the gold. (a) and (b) are not for this design. (b) is the stand-alone\n    31\tCPU question's discount gap, which D263's clause already targets.\n3063:## D262 · 2026-11-02 · The judgement ruler (D3) and its gold are fixed: the gold is reader B's answers; frozen at sha256 636c056d1e4252fa…\n3069:## D263 · 2026-11-02 · The judge is written to the frozen gold's own reasoning (judging-v7, poc-v91); a gap to the gold is closed in the judge, not re-measured\n10660:## D711 · 2026-11-21 · Pilot pair 1 is inconclusive (control double refusal); the comparison is closed; council round 37 recommends a citation-only judge version to Alex\n10825:## D719 · 2026-11-22 · Alex stops the citation line: judging-v12 is not adopted; poc-v118 stays the product\n10839:## D720 · 2026-11-22 · Alex opens the double-count design; the diagnosis finds the class in the frozen product too\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md | sed -n '94,145p'; rg -n 'q01|q02|q19' docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json; shasum -a 256 docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md | sed -n '94,145p'; rg -n 'q01|q02|q19' docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json; shasum -a 256 docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json\"","aggregated_output":"    94\t  gains are ever adjusted; no other date's class changes for credit. When a pair is provisionally improved, **every** gain is reviewed:\n    95\t  both linked answers in force on it (v9's and v14's), deduplicated by judgement id, are read for July's rule B (arithmetic following\n    96\t  from stated inputs; each assumption that sizes the result disclosed and supported) and for H4. A gain whose forecasts carry a material\n    97\t  rule-B failure on which its reconciliation rests earns no credit (counted definite for P). A gain resting on an unsupported CPU\n    98\t  increment: when the increment can be quantified, the forecast with it removed gives the gain's credit-adjusted class; when it cannot,\n    99\t  the gain counts unresolved: **every** such gain of the pair is marked unresolved, and the bounds are then recomputed once across the\n   100\t  pair (round 26: two gains marked one at a time could each look harmless). Each date keeps its actual class in the report beside its\n   101\t  credit-adjusted class; P is recomputed on the credit-adjusted classes. A confirmed CPU double count stays a harm (H4) whatever its\n   102\t  date.\n   103\t- Reported apart, per arm: definite dates that became overlap and those that became clear.\n   104\t\n   105\t**Harm lines (each checked on all three pairs):**\n   106\t- **H1, refusals (round 24):** on **matched reader-days** (a reader judged on that day in both arms of the pair), the attempted reader\n   107\t  judgements and the model refusals among them (first attempts the code refused, terminal refusals included); refusals on linked-only\n   108\t  propagation days reported apart. Harm at **4 or more** refusals above its control on matched reader-days (3 extra is tolerated).\n   109\t  Rationale, frozen before the run: old-2's stored run refused 4 of 1,616 judgements (0.25%), about 0.6 expected per ~250 reader-days, so\n   110\t  4 extra is far above that base rate while up to 3 stays inside ordinary variation. A **linked double refusal** (the corrected answer\n   111\t  refused again: `judgement_refused_twice` stops the day) is a product failure: the verdict is **fail** whatever else holds; a control\n   112\t  double refusal makes the comparison invalid.\n   113\t- **H2, the fixed trajectory:** an unexplained difference in a non-reader request or its substantive output makes the comparison\n   114\t  **invalid** (not a harm: the paired design no longer holds).\n   115\t- **H3, sourcing through the link (round 23):** a figure an answer presents as the market's (consensus, guidance, an analyst estimate)\n   116\t  is followed through the exact request: through the dependency block to the read judgement that stated it, and from there to its dated\n   117\t  outside statement (a company figure such as Bloomberg's $393B total-revenue estimate reaching Data Center through total revenue is the\n   118\t  intended use, if it is named as a company figure). A grounding error: a figure supported only by the system's own forecast, or a\n   119\t  company estimate presented as a segment consensus. **Selection (round 29: the earlier trigger selected 244 of 247 reader answers on\n   120\t  old-2, since the reference field always speaks of the market):** a structural rule from the exact request, the same in both arms\n   121\t  (`worksheet.structural_flag`): a reference amount found among the system's own figures in the request (a read judgement's comparison\n   122\t  point or forecast, or the previous conclusion) and nowhere in the evidence (\"own figure as market\"); or a part question (Data Center,\n   123\t  CPU) whose reference names a consensus with an amount found in the evidence (\"scope check\"); plus every reader answer in force on the\n   124\t  seven fixed dates below. Every selected row is read with its request, the read judgements it was shown and each read judgement's own\n   125\t  request (the trace to the dated outside statement), and the paired arm's conclusion in force that day. On old-2's reader answers the\n   126\t  rule selects 90 of 247 (80 own-figure, 10 scope), many of them cleared on reading (a guide amount written another way, a consensus\n   127\t  named only to say none is supplied). Harm when a linked sequence has a grounding error whose paired control's conclusion in force on\n   128\t  that date does not have the same error type.\n   129\t- **H4, the CPU link:** every post-link v14 answer in the linked sequences is read against the exact CPU conclusion it received and\n   130\t  its control's v14 answer that day. Double counting the CPU forecast is harm. Its effect on P goes only through the credit rule above.\n   131\t- **H5, arithmetic and sourcing (July's rule B):** v9 and v14 at seven fixed dates (07-12, 07-26, 08-09, 08-23, 09-06, 09-13, 09-20) in\n   132\t  all six sequences; the unit is a question-date; Claude reads first, a blind reader second; a disagreement goes to a third,\n   133\t  source-backed read whose class decides. H5 is a separate paired harm count: a linked sequence with at least 2 more material failures\n   134\t  than its control is harm. Its effect on P goes only through the credit rule above (a shared error moves no date).\n   135\t\n   136\t**Verdict precedence:** (1) a **linked double refusal** → fail; (2) **invalid or incomplete** (an infrastructure stop: budget, transport\n   137\tor harness; a control's double refusal; an H2 difference; an arm under 60 resolved dates) → inconclusive; (3) **confirmed harm** (H1, H3,\n   138\tH4 double count, H5) → fail; (4) **untestable** (a control with D_C < 2) → inconclusive, \"the class did not recur\"; (5) **P**: all three\n   139\tpairs improved → pass; any pair not improved → fail; otherwise (some pair undecided) → inconclusive.\n   140\t\n   141\t**The worksheet (pinned before the launch; rounds 24, 28, 29):** built by `worksheet.py` from the six stores and archives after the run,\n   142\tits format and code reviewed and run on the dry outputs before the send, in **two packets**: the P packet (blind: shuffled labels, no\n   143\tarm name, P rows only) for the blind reader, and the H packet (H3, H4, H5 rows, which reveal the arm through their requests); the gain\n   144\tpacket follows the P reading. `verdict.py` takes the read packets themselves: it refuses unless every expected H row (regenerated from\n   145\tthe stores) carries a valid final finding, derives H1 from the dated judge rows of the six summaries, H3–H5 from the read rows, and each\n26:    \"q01\",\n27:    \"q02\",\n34:    \"q19\",\n51:   \"question_id\": \"q01\",\n101:   \"question_id\": \"q02\",\n117:    \"text\": \"Q1 Data Center $75.25bn (i129, i151); Q2 total guided $91bn (i281) at the same share is about $84bn, so the first half is roughly $159bn. My total-revenue view of $400-435bn (q01: i295, i106, i219) at a 92-93% share, with SemiAnalysis expecting second-half data-center compute 20% above consensus (i106, i208), gives $370-400bn.\",\n553:    \"text\": \"Q1 non-GAAP EPS was $1.87 on $81.6bn of revenue (i129); Q2 guidance of $91bn at the same 75% gross margin (i281) gives about $2.09, so consensus needs about $4.73 in the second half. My revenue view of $400-435bn (q01: i295, i106, i219) converted at that second-half rate gives $8.9-9.8, and the $80bn buyback (i301), after $19.3bn bought in Q1 (i131), trims the share count. I bet $9.0-10.0.\",\n581:   \"reasoning\": \"Consensus is $8.69, up 90.2% (i278); an earlier figure was $8.97 (i082). Q1 non-GAAP EPS was $1.87 on $81.6bn of revenue (i129), and Q2 guidance of $91bn at the same 75% gross margin (i281) gives about $2.09, so the first half is about $3.96 and consensus needs about $4.73 in the second half. My revenue view of $400-435bn (q01: i295, i106, i219) converted at that second-half rate gives $8.9-9.8, and the $80bn buyback authorisation (i301) after $19.3bn bought in Q1 (i131) trims the share count. I bet $9.0-10.0, above consensus, with the upside case needing the operating leverage of q10. The risks are memory cost on the Rubin ramp (i217, i252) and the rumoured Vera Rubin lineup delays (i133).\"\n852:   \"question_id\": \"q19\",\n918:    \"text\": \"On my $400-435bn total (q01: i295, i106, i219), Data Center at 92% (i129) is $370-400bn; compute at about 80% of that (i151) is $295-320bn, and rack-level content on NVL72 systems (i216) lifts the product line toward $350bn; Hopper's residual is small, with old GPUs rented rather than bought (i289) and China at zero (i156). I bet $290-350bn.\",\n947:   \"reasoning\": \"No consensus for the line is stated. The Street's $392bn total (i037) at the Q1 Data Center share of 92% (i129), less networking at about a fifth of Data Center (i151), implies roughly $290-320bn of compute, nearly all of it Blackwell and Rubin; Huang's $1 trillion of Blackwell and Vera Rubin sales from March 2026 to the end of 2027 (i079; 'orders' in i036) spans two fiscal years and exceeds consensus total revenue for that window, so it is a bookings claim, not a FY2027 sales figure. On my $400-435bn total (q01: i295, i106, i219), Data Center at 92% (i129) is $370-400bn, compute at about 80% (i151) is $295-320bn, and rack-level content on NVL72 systems (i216) lifts the product line toward $350bn; Hopper's residual is small, with old GPUs rented rather than bought (i289) and China at zero (i156). I bet $290-350bn, in line with the implied reference and tilted up with q01. Huang's $1 trillion (i079) argues for more; the Vera Rubin lineup delay rumour (i133) argues for less.\"\n983:    \"text\": \"A higher FY2027 base of $400-435bn (q01: i295, i106) at the Street's 41% (i090) is $565-615bn; NVIDIA projects the big four's capex rising from $650bn to over $1 trillion in 2027 (i256, i173), a 54% step; Huang's $1 trillion of Blackwell and Rubin sales through 2027 (i079) exceeds consensus total revenue over the window; Rubin racks sell at about twice the GB300 price (i216) and Vera CPUs add a line (i288). Against: the Kyber rack may slip to 2028 (i100) with Oberon-Rubin racks filling the gap (i181), and memory stays tight until 2028 (i252). I bet $560-640bn.\",\n1013:   \"reasoning\": \"Consensus is $554bn (i037), the 41% growth analysts project for next year (i090, i174). A higher FY2027 base of $400-435bn (q01: i295, i106) at the same 41% is $565-615bn. NVIDIA projects the big four's capex rising from $650bn to over $1 trillion in 2027 (i256, i173), a 54% step, Huang's $1 trillion of Blackwell and Rubin sales through 2027 (i079) exceeds consensus total revenue over the window, Rubin racks sell at about twice the GB300 price (i216), and Vera CPUs add a line (i288). Against: SemiAnalysis has the Kyber rack slipping to 2028 with the four-die Rubin Ultra cancelled (i100, i179), which pushes the next product step out of FY2028 and leaves Oberon-Rubin racks to fill it (i181); memory stays tight until 2028 (i252); and Meta's CEO sees slower AI-agent progress (i292). I bet $560-640bn, above consensus, with low confidence eighteen months out.\"\n636c056d1e4252fa759660803067e6ccd67a81067b012905e918aa9dec4005f2  docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json | sed -n '48,150p;849,910p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json | sed -n '48,150p;849,910p'\"","aggregated_output":"    48\t \"unresolved\": 0,\n    49\t \"entries\": [\n    50\t  {\n    51\t   \"question_id\": \"q01\",\n    52\t   \"question\": \"What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?\",\n    53\t   \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    54\t   \"unit\": \"USD\",\n    55\t   \"seed_view_id\": \"v1\",\n    56\t   \"source\": \"reader B\",\n    57\t   \"no_view\": false,\n    58\t   \"reference\": {\n    59\t    \"text\": \"Wall Street consensus $392bn for this fiscal year (i037), consistent with the 82% growth analysts project for this year (i090, i174).\",\n    60\t    \"value\": 392000000000,\n    61\t    \"items\": [\n    62\t     \"i037\",\n    63\t     \"i090\",\n    64\t     \"i174\"\n    65\t    ]\n    66\t   },\n    67\t   \"implied_outcome\": {\n    68\t    \"text\": \"Q1 actual $81.6bn (i280) plus Q2 guidance $91bn plus or minus 2% (i281) give $172.6bn for the first half, so consensus needs about $219bn in the second half. Management says growth rates are accelerating as quarterly revenue approaches $100bn (i295); SemiAnalysis tracks second-half data-center compute 20% above consensus and shipment volumes materially above market estimates (i106, i208, i111); Rubin first shipments are scheduled for Q3 2026 with a volume ramp in Q4 (i219, i105) at about double the GB300 rack price (i216); management targets $20bn of Vera CPU sales by fiscal year-end (i288). A second half of $228-262bn gives $400-435bn.\",\n    69\t    \"low\": 400000000000,\n    70\t    \"high\": 435000000000,\n    71\t    \"yes_no\": null,\n    72\t    \"items\": [\n    73\t     \"i280\",\n    74\t     \"i281\",\n    75\t     \"i295\",\n    76\t     \"i106\",\n    77\t     \"i219\",\n    78\t     \"i216\",\n    79\t     \"i288\"\n    80\t    ]\n    81\t   },\n    82\t   \"direction\": \"above\",\n    83\t   \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n    84\t   \"decisive_items\": [\n    85\t    \"i281\",\n    86\t    \"i295\",\n    87\t    \"i106\",\n    88\t    \"i219\",\n    89\t    \"i037\"\n    90\t   ],\n    91\t   \"against\": {\n    92\t    \"text\": \"Unnamed reports of delays across the upcoming Vera Rubin server lineup (i133); the Kyber report leaves 2026 Rubin on the Oberon rack untouched (i202), but memory stays tight until 2028 (i252) and the guidance assumes no China data-center compute revenue (i281).\",\n    93\t    \"items\": [\n    94\t     \"i133\",\n    95\t     \"i252\"\n    96\t    ]\n    97\t   },\n    98\t   \"reasoning\": \"The Street expects $392bn for FY2027 (i037), which is the 82% growth analysts project (i090). The known half is $172.6bn: Q1 revenue $81.6bn (i280) plus Q2 guidance of $91bn plus or minus 2% (i281), so consensus needs about $219bn in the second half. Management told a Morgan Stanley roadshow that growth rates are accelerating as quarterly revenue approaches $100bn (i295), and SemiAnalysis tracks second-half data-center compute 20% above consensus with shipment volumes materially above market estimates (i106, i208, i111). Rubin ships from Q3 2026 with the volume ramp in Q4 (i219, i105) at about $7.8m a rack against $4m for GB300 (i216), and management targets $20bn of Vera CPU sales by fiscal year-end (i288). A second half of $228-262bn gives $400-435bn, above consensus. The risks are supply, with memory tight until 2028 (i252), rumoured delays across the Vera Rubin lineup (i133), and guidance that assumes no China data-center compute revenue (i281).\"\n    99\t  },\n   100\t  {\n   101\t   \"question_id\": \"q02\",\n   102\t   \"question\": \"What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\",\n   103\t   \"kind\": \"asset\",\n   104\t   \"unit\": \"USD\",\n   105\t   \"seed_view_id\": \"v2\",\n   106\t   \"source\": \"reader B\",\n   107\t   \"no_view\": false,\n   108\t   \"reference\": {\n   109\t    \"text\": \"No segment consensus is stated; consensus total revenue of $392bn (i037) at the Q1 Data Center share of 92% of sales (i129) implies about $360bn.\",\n   110\t    \"value\": 360000000000,\n   111\t    \"items\": [\n   112\t     \"i037\",\n   113\t     \"i129\"\n   114\t    ]\n   115\t   },\n   116\t   \"implied_outcome\": {\n   117\t    \"text\": \"Q1 Data Center $75.25bn (i129, i151); Q2 total guided $91bn (i281) at the same share is about $84bn, so the first half is roughly $159bn. My total-revenue view of $400-435bn (q01: i295, i106, i219) at a 92-93% share, with SemiAnalysis expecting second-half data-center compute 20% above consensus (i106, i208), gives $370-400bn.\",\n   118\t    \"low\": 370000000000,\n   119\t    \"high\": 400000000000,\n   120\t    \"yes_no\": null,\n   121\t    \"items\": [\n   122\t     \"i129\",\n   123\t     \"i151\",\n   124\t     \"i281\",\n   125\t     \"i106\",\n   126\t     \"i208\",\n   127\t     \"i295\"\n   128\t    ]\n   129\t   },\n   130\t   \"direction\": \"above\",\n   131\t   \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n   132\t   \"decisive_items\": [\n   133\t    \"i129\",\n   134\t    \"i281\",\n   135\t    \"i106\",\n   136\t    \"i208\",\n   137\t    \"i295\"\n   138\t   ],\n   139\t   \"against\": {\n   140\t    \"text\": \"Unnamed reports of delays across the Vera Rubin server lineup (i133); the guidance assumes zero China data-center compute revenue (i281, i156).\",\n   141\t    \"items\": [\n   142\t     \"i133\",\n   143\t     \"i281\"\n   144\t    ]\n   145\t   },\n   146\t   \"reasoning\": \"No segment consensus is stated; the Street's $392bn total (i037) at the Q1 Data Center share of 92% of sales (i129) implies about $360bn. Q1 Data Center was $75.25bn, up 92% year on year and 21% quarter on quarter (i129, i151), and Q2 guidance of $91bn (i281) at the same share is about $84bn, so the first half is roughly $159bn. SemiAnalysis expects second-half data-center compute 20% above the Street (i106, i208), management describes accelerating growth toward $100bn quarters (i295), and Rubin volume ramps in Q4 at nearly twice the rack price (i219, i216). My total-revenue view of $400-435bn at a 92-93% share gives $370-400bn, above the implied consensus. The guidance assumes zero China data-center compute (i281, i156), so any H200 permission (i270) is upside not in these numbers; the rumoured Vera Rubin lineup delays (i133) are the downside.\"\n   147\t  },\n   148\t  {\n   149\t   \"question_id\": \"q03\",\n   150\t   \"question\": \"What non-GAAP gross margin will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\",\n   849\t   \"reasoning\": \"NVIDIA's roadmap has Kyber and Rubin Ultra in 2027 (i277, i101). SemiAnalysis reports, with a mechanism, that the Kyber NVL144 rack slipped more than twelve months to 2028 because the PCB midplane cannot yet be manufactured (i100, i177, i189, i196; one claim carried by many outlets), that the NVL72x2 stopgap was scrapped after customer pushback (i102, i178), that the four-die Rubin Ultra was dropped for a two-die part (i179, i192, i200), that CPO-NVSwitch slips to the Feynman generation (i180, i201), and that NVIDIA will cover the gap by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks (i181). NVIDIA's reply is three words, 'Our roadmap is intact' (i101, i193, i211), and Tom's Hardware notes it is unclear whether the original or an already updated roadmap is meant (i197); suppliers' shares fell on the report (i177, i194). A 2027 product with a specific delay report needs only a small slip to miss December 31, 2027, and the question also requires the Kyber NVL144 form. I bet no, with low confidence: the material does not settle it, and the company's denial (i101, i247) is the strongest item against.\"\n   850\t  },\n   851\t  {\n   852\t   \"question_id\": \"q19\",\n   853\t   \"question\": \"How much revenue will NVIDIA report from standalone Vera CPUs in FY2027?\",\n   854\t   \"kind\": \"asset\",\n   855\t   \"unit\": \"USD\",\n   856\t   \"seed_view_id\": \"v19\",\n   857\t   \"source\": \"reader B\",\n   858\t   \"no_view\": false,\n   859\t   \"reference\": {\n   860\t    \"text\": \"Management's own figure: $20bn of stand-alone CPU sales this year (i095), by the end of this fiscal year (i288), nearly $20bn with no period (i080): one claim retold three times.\",\n   861\t    \"value\": 20000000000,\n   862\t    \"items\": [\n   863\t     \"i288\",\n   864\t     \"i095\",\n   865\t     \"i080\"\n   866\t    ]\n   867\t   },\n   868\t   \"implied_outcome\": {\n   869\t    \"text\": \"The first stand-alone CPU launches this fall (i096), so the revenue falls in roughly two quarters; at Morgan Stanley's $5,000 per Vera CPU (i218), $20bn is four million units. Demand signals are real: Perplexity plans to deploy Vera in production (i262, i291) and Intel's CEO sees the CPU:GPU ratio in inference moving toward 1:1 (i009); but NVIDIA chose Intel Xeon 6 as host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8 (i008). I bet $8-18bn, below the $20bn ambition.\",\n   870\t    \"low\": 8000000000,\n   871\t    \"high\": 18000000000,\n   872\t    \"yes_no\": null,\n   873\t    \"items\": [\n   874\t     \"i096\",\n   875\t     \"i218\",\n   876\t     \"i262\",\n   877\t     \"i291\",\n   878\t     \"i009\",\n   879\t     \"i008\"\n   880\t    ]\n   881\t   },\n   882\t   \"direction\": \"below\",\n   883\t   \"confidence\": \"low\",\n   884\t   \"decisive_items\": [\n   885\t    \"i288\",\n   886\t    \"i095\",\n   887\t    \"i096\",\n   888\t    \"i218\"\n   889\t   ],\n   890\t   \"against\": {\n   891\t    \"text\": \"Management expects $20bn by fiscal year-end (i288, i095), and Intel's CEO describes inference moving toward one CPU per GPU (i009).\",\n   892\t    \"items\": [\n   893\t     \"i288\",\n   894\t     \"i009\"\n   895\t    ]\n   896\t   },\n   897\t   \"reasoning\": \"The reference is management's own number: $20bn of stand-alone CPU sales this year (i095), by the end of this fiscal year (i288), nearly $20bn with no period (i080), one claim retold three times. The first stand-alone CPU launches this fall (i096), so the revenue falls in roughly two quarters, and at Morgan Stanley's $5,000 per Vera CPU (i218) $20bn is four million units. Demand signals are real: Perplexity plans to deploy Vera in production after a 1.5x result on agentic coding (i262, i291), and Intel's CEO sees the CPU:GPU ratio in inference moving from 1:8 toward 1:1 (i009); but NVIDIA itself chose Intel Xeon 6 as host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8 (i008). Lean cautious on a product with no shipments yet: I bet $8-18bn, below the $20bn ambition, with the top of the range reachable if the fall ramp is steep. Management's $20bn (i288, i095) is the strongest item against.\"\n   898\t  },\n   899\t  {\n   900\t   \"question_id\": \"q20\",\n   901\t   \"question\": \"How much revenue will NVIDIA report from Blackwell and Rubin products in FY2027?\",\n   902\t   \"kind\": \"asset\",\n   903\t   \"unit\": \"USD\",\n   904\t   \"seed_view_id\": \"v20\",\n   905\t   \"source\": \"reader B\",\n   906\t   \"no_view\": false,\n   907\t   \"reference\": {\n   908\t    \"text\": \"None stated for the line; consensus total revenue $392bn (i037) at the Q1 Data Center share of 92% (i129), less networking at about a fifth of Data Center (i151), implies about $290-320bn of compute, nearly all Blackwell and Rubin. Huang's $1 trillion of Blackwell and Vera Rubin sales from March 2026 to end-2027 (i079; 'orders' in i036) spans two fiscal years and exceeds consensus total revenue for that window, so it is not a FY2027 sales figure.\",\n   909\t    \"value\": 300000000000,\n   910\t    \"items\": [\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
