{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'ls -l poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-{1,2,3}/prod/{views,archive}.sqlite /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/{linked-1-copy,control-1-copy}/{views,archive}.sqlite'","aggregated_output":"-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  wheel  295227392 Nov 21 15:48 /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/control-1-copy/archive.sqlite\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  wheel   14856192 Nov 21 15:48 /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/control-1-copy/views.sqlite\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  wheel  299663360 Nov 21 16:40 /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/linked-1-copy/archive.sqlite\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  wheel   19124224 Nov 21 16:40 /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/linked-1-copy/views.sqlite\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  staff  336748544 Nov 18 01:20 poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-1/prod/archive.sqlite\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  staff   19767296 Nov 18 01:20 poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-1/prod/views.sqlite\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  staff  282230784 Nov 18 01:20 poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod/archive.sqlite\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  staff   18612224 Nov 18 01:20 poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod/views.sqlite\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  staff  297226240 Nov 18 01:20 poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-3/prod/archive.sqlite\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  staff   19165184 Nov 18 01:20 poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-3/prod/views.sqlite\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PILOT-RESULT.md | sed -n '18,65p'; sed -n '3063,3076p;10660,10675p' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; rg -n 'i288|i295|i219|i106|i208|i037' docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/packet-v1.json | head -18\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PILOT-RESULT.md | sed -n '18,65p'; sed -n '3063,3076p;10660,10675p' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; rg -n 'i288|i295|i219|i106|i208|i037' docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/packet-v1.json | head -18\"","aggregated_output":"    18\t\n    19\tThese come from one pair and one draw. They are not the registered blind reading.\n    20\t\n    21\t| Line | linked-1 | control-1 | How it was read |\n    22\t|---|---|---|---|\n    23\t| P: Data Center above total revenue, 07-10..09-07 | parser 3 of 60; **0 real** | parser 28 of 60; the 5 sampled are real | Codex read all 12 linked dates the parser flagged across the window; Claude read 4 of them and 5 random control dates |\n    24\t| H4: CPU double count in total revenue | **4 answers** (07-15, 07-16, 07-17, 07-18; in force 07-15..07-19) | no CPU link | Claude read all 71 post-link v14 answers; Codex did a shuffled second read with dates hidden (exploratory, because the list was in the brief); both lists agree |\n    25\t| H1: reader refusals on the 200 matched reader-days | 5 | 1 | an excess of 4, at the harm line |\n    26\t\n    27\t### P\n    28\t- In linked-1, Data Center is computed as a share of v14's total. Example from 07-22: \"About $352 billion: v14's approximately $387.3\n    29\t  billion … multiplied by 91%\".\n    30\t- Linked-1's parser hits are artefacts: the parser took the total's amount as the Data Center figure.\n    31\t- The safe claim is that the link kept the two forecasts consistent in the readings checked. Removal of the class is not shown.\n    32\t\n    33\t### H4\n    34\t- Each double-counting answer adds $19–19.5B to a total of about $387B built from company guidance. That addition lifts the total\n    35\t  above $400B.\n    36\t- On 07-20 the judge reversed it itself (\"included within, not added to\").\n    37\t- The link behind it, v14 reads v8 (\"stand-alone CPU sales\"), had been flagged in D705 as running against the tie-break's direction.\n    38\t\n    39\t### H1\n    40\t- A resumed control would make (v21, 09-08) a matched day and add the control's two refusals. That lowers the excess to 2 or 3.\n    41\t\n    42\t### Refused answers\n    43\t- Every refused judge answer is an id-membership error: an id outside the list its field allows. Count: 17 in 5,408 distinct\n    44\t  archived attempts (old-1, old-2, old-3, and the fresh calls of both pilot arms).\n    45\t- All 5,389 committed answers fit those lists.\n    46\t- 6 of linked-1's 7 fresh reader refusals mix up which list an id belongs to:\n    47\t  - the dependency `v14` in evidence citations, twice;\n    48\t  - theme ids in `cites_views` beside a valid dependency, four times.\n    49\t- The closing instruction of every request already forbids theme ids in `cites_views` (`judging.py` lines 180–182). The answer format\n    50\t  accepts any string in these fields.\n    51\t\n    52\t## Council round 37 (`round37-brief.md`, `codex-r37.md`, `claude-r37.md`, `round37b-brief.md`, `codex-r37b.md`)\n    53\t\n    54\t- **Consensus: A.**\n    55\t  - C (resume control-1 from 09-08) is exploratory at most. The stop and the control's lead were seen before the rule change was\n    56\t    proposed, and a resume would move H1 across its line through the very refusals that invalidated the control.\n    57\t  - B (a redraw) selects a replacement after seeing the stop and repeats the risk.\n    58\t  - Claude first proposed C; it withdrew C once linked-1's H1 numbers showed the effect.\n    59\t- **A resume-once rule** for a double refusal may be used only in a future comparison registered before its send. It applies to both\n    60\t  arms, keeps the original refusals in the matched-day count, and is proven on stored data first (the runner overwrites a resumed\n    61\t  day's call range today).\n    62\t\n    63\t## Recommendation to Alex (consensus)\n    64\t\n    65\tOpen a judge version that changes the citation format only:\n## D262 · 2026-11-02 · The judgement ruler (D3) and its gold are fixed: the gold is reader B's answers; frozen at sha256 636c056d1e4252fa…\n\n- Alex, 2026-11-02, after reading the two readers' reasoning on every disagreement (readers A and B differed on 3 of 23 questions by range or yes/no and on 12 of 20 direction labels; the system disagreed with the draft on the total revenue and Vera CPU questions in every draw): \"那就不要理A了；我们就go with B 好吧，定下尺子金表\".\n- Fixed by that decision: the ruler `research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md` (unit: a gold question at the window end 2026-07-14; material: golden-v5's 309 items by sha; a numeric question agrees when the gold's implied range overlaps the band of the rung the judge chose, a yes/no question when the answers match; the two null baselines printed with every score); the question list and the must_open mapping as built (`questions-v1.json`, 22 seeds + the Ohio guarantee); the gold `gold-v1.json` = reader B's 23 answers verbatim (B: a fresh-context Claude reader, model-authored, 3 questions marked no material: CoWoS capacity, semiconductor growth, SK Telecom), sha256 `636c056d1e4252fa759660803067e6ccd67a81067b012905e918aa9dec4005f2` in `gold-v1.sha256` and in `score_judgement.py`, which refuses any other. Reader A (Codex) stays beside it as the control record (`reader-A.json`, `reconcile-v1.md`): its distance from B is the stick's own noise. Claude's two proposals of the afternoon (a three-level score when readers split; toss-up questions excluded) were not adopted: one range per question, every question counts. From here D261 applies: nothing in the ruler or the gold changes; a defect is a dated record in `corrections-v1.json`.\n- First measurement on the frozen gold (`score-r4-identity.txt`, arm 4, three draws, judging-v5): judged questions agreeing 13/19, 15/18, 12/17; the lookup baseline (the rung containing the gold's reference figure) 9/15, 8/14, 8/14; always-neutral 5/11, 5/10, 5/10. Disagreeing in every draw: FY2027 total revenue (gold $400–435 billion, judge neutral by consensus lookup) and Vera CPU revenue (gold $8–18 billion, judge the top rung on management's $20 billion at face value); in two of three: the financing-commitments total (gold $25–60 billion, judge $10–20 billion). Never judged in any draw: NVIDIA's own capex (q12); the operating margin in two draws (q10): evidence never reached them, counted apart as \"no judgement\". The new judge (`judging-v6`, D260) has no run to score yet; a run on `poc-v90` is Alex's call.\n\n## D263 · 2026-11-02 · The judge is written to the frozen gold's own reasoning (judging-v7, poc-v91); a gap to the gold is closed in the judge, not re-measured\n\n- Alex, after the first score on the frozen gold was reported as the system being \"wrong\" on some questions: \"不是啊什么叫错？我不是和你说了就按照claude的标准来就好了嘛\". The gold is Claude's (reader B's) standard by D262; a disagreement is the distance between the judge and that standard, to be closed in the judge's rule, not a verdict to be measured again.\n- What reader B does that judging-v6 did not, read from B's answers on the questions that differed (`judgement-gold/reconcile-v1.md`, `gold-v1.json`): carries the guided growth forward rather than annualising the last quarter flat (Data Center, total revenue); discounts a management target for a product with no shipments by default (Vera CPU: two selling quarters, four million units at $5,000, the company's own choice of another CPU); sizes an announced but unsized commitment from its disclosed comparable (the Firmus backstop from Sharon's); derives a figure no one states from the accounting identities in the evidence (capex from operating less free cash flow); keeps linked conclusions consistent (China share against the H200 authorization theme). Written into `judging-v7.txt` as five clauses (v6 3,782 → 4,924 characters; reason: each clause is a behaviour of the frozen standard the judge lacked), named by `poc-v91`; schema and code unchanged.\n- Proof on the stored requests of the questions whose arm-4 judgement differed from the gold (`proof-judging-v7a.txt`, `proof-judging-v7.txt`; one send each, cents): total revenue rung 3 → 2 (about $421 billion from the guided growth on FY2026's base; gold $400–435 billion), Data Center 3 → 2 (about $372 billion; gold $370–400 billion), Vera CPU 1 → 2 ($12 to below $20 billion, the target discounted for the volume not in hand; gold $8–18 billion), DRAM prices 3 → 2 (about +15%; gold +10–35%), financing total stays 4 ($33.5–39.8 billion; gold $25–60 billion), the Ohio guarantee stays Yes; China share stays below 1% in draw 1, consistent with that draw's H200 theme judged No (the gold has the authorization likely and the share 1–8%; the consistency clause ties the share to the theme, so it moves with it). Six of the seven re-sent questions now land in the gold's band; the end-to-end agreement under judging-v7 needs a run on `poc-v91`, which is Alex's call. A placement gap seen on the way: the Vera CPU question never held the fall-launch statement (filed under the Rubin launch questions), so the judge could not count selling quarters from evidence; the default-discount clause covers it, the filing is a D2 class to count.\n\n## D264 · 2026-11-02 · The extraction reads the day's open questions with their takes lines and files the statements itself (extraction v72, poc-v92): the one-decider architecture Alex chose is built and measured against poc-v91 on the frozen ruler\n\n## D711 · 2026-11-21 · Pilot pair 1 is inconclusive (control double refusal); the comparison is closed; council round 37 recommends a citation-only judge version to Alex\n\n- **Alex's words** (2026-11-21, on the stopped control): \"好你跑一次council 看trace 同意了就开始下一步\".\n- **Registered verdict:** inconclusive. control-1 (poc-v118) stopped on 09-08 when v21's answer and its retry each cited an id outside\n  the listed evidence (`e362` for `e3620`; `e3111` beside `e3110`/`e3112`), PAID-DESIGN.md §4 (2). linked-1 completed with every\n  acceptance check true. control-1 equals old-2 on every H2 table through 09-07. Spend $1.70 of $2.50.\n- **Exploratory, one pair, one draw, beside the verdict** (`whole-window/PILOT-RESULT.md`):\n  - P: the parser counts 3 of 60 dates (linked-1) against 28 of 60 (control-1) on 07-10..09-07. All 12 flagged linked dates were read:\n    none has Data Center above total revenue (parser artefacts). The 5 sampled control dates are real.\n  - H4: four linked v14 answers (07-15..07-18) add v8's stand-alone CPU forecast to a total built from company guidance, read by Claude\n    and, shuffled, by Codex.\n  - H1: 5 linked against 1 control refusal on the 200 matched reader-days, at the harm line.\n  - Every refused judge answer seen is an id-membership error: 17 in 5,408 distinct attempts. 6 of linked-1's 7 fresh reader refusals\n    put an id in the wrong list, though the request's closing instruction forbids it.\n- **Council round 37** (`round37-brief.md`, `codex-r37.md`, `claude-r37.md`, `round37b-brief.md`, `codex-r37b.md`), consensus A:\n  - pair 1 is closed, with no redraw (B) and no resume (C);\n600:   \"id\": \"i037\",\n1727:   \"id\": \"i106\",\n3411:   \"id\": \"i208\",\n3592:   \"id\": \"i219\",\n4694:   \"id\": \"i288\",\n4804:   \"id\": \"i295\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib,json,collections\nscratch=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\ndef db(a,name=\\\"views\\\"):\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{a}/prod\\\") if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else scratch/(a+\\\"-copy\\\")\n c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/(name+\\\".sqlite\\\"))+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\ndef req(a,id):\n row=db(a,\\\"archive\\\").execute(\\\"select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n return json.loads(row[0])[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"],json.loads(row[1])\nfor a,ids in [(\\\"old-1\\\",[354,978,1142,1208,3464,4163,853]),(\\\"linked-1\\\",[8752,8756,8759,8763,8766,8758,8761,8770])]:\n co=db(a)\n for id in ids:\n  r=co.execute(\\\"select * from judgements where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n  print(a,id,r[\\\"view_id\\\"],r[\\\"publication_day\\\"],r[\\\"rung\\\"],r[\\\"reference\\\"],r[\\\"implied_outcome\\\"],r[\\\"why_changed\\\"],sep=\\\" | \\\")\nif True:\n for a in [\\\"old-1\\\",\\\"old-2\\\",\\\"old-3\\\",\\\"linked-1\\\",\\\"control-1\\\"]:\n  c=db(a)\n  print(a)\n  for v,q in c.execute(\\\"select view_id,question from views where object_id=\\\\x27NVDA\\\\x27 and (lower(question) like \\\\x27%stand-alone%cpu%\\\\x27 or lower(question) like \\\\x27%standalone%cpu%\\\\x27 or lower(question) like \\\\x27%vera%cpu%\\\\x27)\\\"):\n   for x in c.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,implied_outcome from judgements where view_id=? and publication_day between \\\\x272026-07-13\\\\x27 and \\\\x272026-07-20\\\\x27 and call_id>? order by seq\\\",(v,0 if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)):\n    print(v,tuple(x))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib,json,collections\nscratch=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\ndef db(a,name=\\\"views\\\"):\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{a}/prod\\\") if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else scratch/(a+\\\"-copy\\\")\n c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/(name+\\\".sqlite\\\"))+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\ndef req(a,id):\n row=db(a,\\\"archive\\\").execute(\\\"select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n return json.loads(row[0])[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"],json.loads(row[1])\nfor a,ids in [(\\\"old-1\\\",[354,978,1142,1208,3464,4163,853]),(\\\"linked-1\\\",[8752,8756,8759,8763,8766,8758,8761,8770])]:\n co=db(a)\n for id in ids:\n  r=co.execute(\\\"select * from judgements where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n  print(a,id,r[\\\"view_id\\\"],r[\\\"publication_day\\\"],r[\\\"rung\\\"],r[\\\"reference\\\"],r[\\\"implied_outcome\\\"],r[\\\"why_changed\\\"],sep=\\\" | \\\")\nif True:\n for a in [\\\"old-1\\\",\\\"old-2\\\",\\\"old-3\\\",\\\"linked-1\\\",\\\"control-1\\\"]:\n  c=db(a)\n  print(a)\n  for v,q in c.execute(\\\"select view_id,question from views where object_id=\\\\x27NVDA\\\\x27 and (lower(question) like \\\\x27%stand-alone%cpu%\\\\x27 or lower(question) like \\\\x27%standalone%cpu%\\\\x27 or lower(question) like \\\\x27%vera%cpu%\\\\x27)\\\"):\n   for x in c.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,implied_outcome from judgements where view_id=? and publication_day between \\\\x272026-07-13\\\\x27 and \\\\x272026-07-20\\\\x27 and call_id>? order by seq\\\",(v,0 if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)):\n    print(v,tuple(x))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"old-1 | 354 | v4 | 2026-07-07 | 2 | Wall Street analysts estimate $392 billion in FY2027 revenue (e28), also expressed as 82% FY2027 growth (e51). The $392 billion reference is the lower boundary of rung 2. | About $425 billion for FY2027: retain the prior roughly $420 billion central outlook supported by reported Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance, then allow a limited uplift for the $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast because the planned fall launch leaves few selling quarters and shipments are not yet shown. The resulting estimate remains below $431.2 billion. | The rung remains 2, but the implied outlook is modestly higher than the previous roughly $420 billion estimate because management’s specific $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast is new evidence. It is substantially discounted for the late planned launch and lack of volume in hand; new product-transition and China-substitution risks temper, but do not erase, that increase.\nold-1 | 978 | v4 | 2026-07-15 | 1 | Bloomberg-tracked analyst estimates of about $393 billion for FY2027 (e234) are the market reference, in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). | About $435 billion: starting from the prior estimate of roughly $430 billion after applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% second-half data-center compute upside to consensus (e154), I allow a net few billion dollars for a discounted share of the $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast (e65), given the planned fall launch and limited selling quarters (e64). | The prior estimate was roughly $430 billion, below the rung 1 threshold. The FY2027 stand-alone CPU sales forecast, discounted for the fall launch and execution risk, raises the implied estimate by enough to cross that boundary; this changes both the implied figure and the rung.\nold-1 | 1142 | v4 | 2026-07-16 | 1 | Bloomberg-tracked FY2027 revenue estimates of about $393 billion; this is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). | Roughly $435 billion: about $393 billion consensus plus the previously derived roughly $37 billion SemiAnalysis second-half data-center compute uplift and a discounted few billion net contribution from the $20 billion stand-alone CPU target. The newly reported projects and supply outlook do not support a further quantified adjustment, while rack delays temper the estimate. | The rung and roughly $435 billion implied outcome are unchanged, but the supporting evidence has changed: new sovereign-AI and TSMC growth evidence adds support, while the funded but undated Noetra project and the further reported rack constraint refine the upside and execution-risk picture.\nold-1 | 1208 | v4 | 2026-07-17 | 1 | Bloomberg-tracked analyst expectations of roughly $392–$393 billion for FY2027; rung 2 (Bullish). | About $435 billion: retain the prior roughly $393 billion consensus base, add about $37 billion implied by SemiAnalysis's estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue is 20% above Wall Street's expectation, and add only a few billion net for the $20 billion stand-alone CPU target after discounting it for execution and the short selling window. This is above the $431.2 billion rung 1 threshold. | The forecast remains about $435 billion and in rung 1. The added evidence reinforces both the existing demand case and the known execution risks, but supplies no new, sufficiently sized and timed FY2027 revenue change; repeated reports of already-known rack issues do not change the conclusion.\nold-1 | 3464 | v4 | 2026-08-14 | 1 | Wall Street estimates of roughly $392–393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). | About $440 billion: carry the reported Q1 and guided Q2 growth trajectory through FY2027, incorporate the analyst Q3 estimate, and apply the 20% second-half upside only to data-center compute, with the separately forecast $20 billion standalone-CPU contribution. This places the forecast above $431.2 billion without treating multi-period orders or SpaceX’s later capacity target as FY2027 revenue. | The rung and approximately $440 billion forecast remain, but recent evidence adds a more specific SpaceX capacity and potential-orders case, alongside concrete competing-chip plans and a current-quarter China exclusion. These change the supporting facts and the balance of risks, even though they do not justify counting the prospective SpaceX spending as FY2027 NVIDIA revenue or moving the forecast across a rung boundary.\nold-1 | 4163 | v4 | 2026-08-21 | 1 | Wall Street’s roughly $392–$393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference; that sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). | About $434 billion: Q1’s $81.6 billion, BofA’s $94–$95 billion Q2 estimate, its $107–$108 billion potential Q3 guidance, and roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 imply about $404 billion before allowing roughly $20 billion for standalone CPU sales and a heavily discounted ~$10 billion FY2027 contribution from SpaceX-related demand. | The forecast moves above the rung-1 boundary on a higher implied outcome: new specific SpaceX capacity and chip-spending estimates, alongside reported AI capex, strengthen the case for FY2027 demand beyond the prior roughly $420 billion estimate. The large SpaceX target is discounted because it extends through end-2027 and does not state FY2027 shipments.\nold-1 | 853 | v4 | 2026-07-14 | 2 | Wall Street consensus is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). | Approximately $430 billion: apply SemiAnalysis’s 20% uplift only to second-half FY2027 data-center compute, implying roughly $37 billion above the approximately $393 billion consensus total. This remains below the $431.2 billion rung 1 threshold. | The rung remains 2 and the implied outcome remains around $430 billion, but the current assessment incorporates the updated China export-controls theme and new, specific evidence on H200 approvals and shipment volumes, as well as the reported product delays and cancellations. These alter the balance of supporting facts without moving the estimate across a rung boundary.\nlinked-1 | 8752 | v14 | 2026-07-14 | 3 | Bloomberg projects $393 billion of FY2027 sales, placing the market reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Wall Street's separate “well over $300 billion” expectation is less specific. | About $387 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 and $91 billion guided for Q2 imply roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that growth forward gives approximately $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, totaling about $387 billion. | The rung and run-rate estimate remain unchanged, but the supporting evidence has changed: the disclosed 2 million-GPU AWS deployment plan adds a quantified demand signal, while the reported HBM4 ramp slowdown and updated customer-compliance and export-control measures add more specific supply and China-related risks.\nlinked-1 | 8756 | v14 | 2026-07-15 | 2 | Bloomberg projects $393 billion of FY2027 sales, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Wall Street's reported “well over $300 billion” expectation is too imprecise to place more narrowly. | Carrying the sequential growth from Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion to Q2 guidance of $91 billion forward implies about $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, or roughly $387 billion for FY2027. Adding the dependency's approximately $19.5 billion stand-alone CPU forecast gives about $407 billion. | The rung changes from 3 to 2: the current stand-alone CPU forecast in dependency v8 adds about $19.5 billion to the approximately $387 billion revenue run-rate, taking the implied total above $400 billion.\nlinked-1 | 8759 | v14 | 2026-07-16 | 2 | Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection is the clearest quantified market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). | About $407 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, with Q2's sequential growth carried into Q3 and Q4, implies roughly $387 billion; adding the dependency's approximately $19.5 billion stand-alone CPU forecast gives about $407 billion. | The rung and central implied estimate remain near the previous conclusion, but the updated AI-capex theme now provides additional support for the above-$400 billion forecast; the updated export-controls theme is a counterweight.\nlinked-1 | 8763 | v14 | 2026-07-17 | 2 | Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 revenue projection is the market reference and sits in rung 3. | Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies about $387 billion for FY2027 if Q2's sequential growth rate carries through Q3 and Q4. Adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate as incremental implies about $406 billion. | The rung remains 2, but the implied estimate changes to about $406 billion using the current dependency conclusion of approximately $19 billion for stand-alone CPU sales, rather than the earlier approximately $19.5 billion estimate. Updated China-policy and AI-capex themes also change the supporting outlook.\nlinked-1 | 8766 | v14 | 2026-07-18 | 2 | Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). | Applying the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate (about 11.5%) to Q2's $91 billion guidance for Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $387 billion for FY2027. Adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate gives an implied total of about $406 billion, in rung 2. | The rung and implied estimate remain about $406 billion, but new, more specific HBM-supply evidence and the UAE export-tier change alter the key risk balance supporting that estimate.\nlinked-1 | 8758 | v9 | 2026-07-15 | 3 | Bloomberg’s FY2027 total-revenue projection of $393 billion, translated using the latest 92% Data Center mix to approximately $362 billion; this is rung 3. | Approximately $356 billion for FY2027 Data Center revenue: carry the sequential growth from Q1 total revenue of $81.6 billion to the $91 billion Q2 guide into Q3 and Q4, producing about $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue, then apply the observed 92% Data Center mix. This is below $400 billion and consistent with v14’s higher total forecast because its approximately $19.5 billion stand-alone CPU contribution is outside Data Center. | The rung remains 3 and the implied Data Center figure remains about $356 billion, but the supporting forecast context has changed: dependency v14 now forecasts roughly $407 billion of total FY2027 revenue including a separate stand-alone CPU contribution, and recent order and demand evidence adds upside without moving the Data Center estimate across a rung boundary.\nlinked-1 | 8761 | v9 | 2026-07-16 | 3 | Bloomberg’s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, converted using the latest 92% Data Center mix, implies about $361 billion of Data Center revenue and sits in rung 3. | About $376 billion: carrying the $81.6 billion Q1 result and $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide forward implies roughly $387 billion for FY2027; applying the 92% Data Center mix gives about $356 billion, then adding v14’s approximately $19.5 billion stand-alone CPU forecast as incremental Data Center revenue gives about $376 billion. | The rung remains 3, but the implied Data Center forecast rises from about $356 billion to about $376 billion because the current v14 forecast adds approximately $19.5 billion of FY2027 stand-alone CPU revenue to the prior run-rate estimate.\nlinked-1 | 8770 | v14 | 2026-07-20 | 3 | Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue projection of $393 billion is the market reference; it sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). | Using Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the 11.5% sequential increase carried forward gives approximately $101.5 billion for Q3 and $113.2 billion for Q4, or about $387 billion for FY2027. The approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate is included within, not added to, total company revenue. | The rung changes from 2 to 3: carrying Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth forward gives about $387 billion, below $400 billion. The stand-alone CPU estimate is part of total revenue and is not added again to the company-wide run-rate; the new evidence on export access and supply risks does not quantify an offset large enough to change that estimate.\nold-1\nold-2\nv8 (590, '2026-07-13', 3, 'Approximately $18–19 billion in FY2027: discount the $20 billion forecast because the evidence shows prospective use and offers to customers, but no shipped volume, deployment scale, or quantified sales run-rate.')\nv8 (721, '2026-07-14', 3, 'About $18–19 billion in FY2027: discount the $20 billion target because the evidence shows an offer to cloud customers and customer plans, but no disclosed shipment volume, deployment scale, or sales run-rate to establish the target in full.')\nv8 (853, '2026-07-15', 3, 'About $19.5 billion in FY2027: discount NVIDIA’s roughly $20 billion target because no shipment volume or deployment scale is shown, while allowing for the customer interest and favorable updated demand/access themes.')\nv8 (1018, '2026-07-16', 3, 'About $19.5 billion in FY2027: start from the $20 billion company forecast and apply a modest execution discount because the rollout and named customer interest are not accompanied by shipment volumes, deployment scale, or a sales run-rate. This is below $20 billion but above $15 billion, so it implies rung 3.')\nv8 (1091, '2026-07-17', 3, 'Approximately $19 billion in FY2027—an analytical estimate just below the company’s roughly $20 billion target, discounted because the evidence describes offers and customer intent but no quantified shipments or deployments.')\nv8 (1131, '2026-07-19', 3, \"Roughly $19 billion in FY2027: a modest discount to NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion target because the evidence does not show shipment volume or quantified customer deployments sufficient to support the full target. This falls in rung 3 ($15 billion to less than $20 billion).\")\nv8 (1214, '2026-07-20', 3, \"Approximately $19 billion in FY2027, near but below $20 billion. This discounts NVIDIA's target because shipment volume is not shown to be in hand, while the customer interest and reported cloud offering activity provide some support for approaching it.\")\nold-3\nlinked-1\ncontrol-1\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
