quential total-revenue growth implied by Q2 actuals and Q3 guidance gives roughly $112B for Q4.\n8922 09-04 2 The Q1–Q4 run-rate is about $376–379B: $75.25B plus $89.0B, about $99.9B for Q3 from the $108B total outlook at a 92.5% Data Center mix, and about $112B for Q4 carrying roughly 12% sequential growth. Applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 beat forecast to the roughly $212B H2 run-rate as a proxy implies about $418B for FY2027; discounted for the forecast’s July timing and unstated consensus baseline, the likely outcome is just over $400B.\n8925 09-05 2 About $418B: Q1 ($75.25B) plus Q2 ($89B), estimated Q3 Data Center revenue of about $99.9B ($108B total guidance at the recent 92.5% mix), and a roughly $112B Q4 base carrying forward Q3’s sequential growth imply about $376B before the H2 uplift. Applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 outperformance forecast to the roughly $212B H2 base yields about $254B of H2 revenue and approximately $418B for the year.\n8930 09-06 2 About $426B: FY2027 Q1 and Q2 contribute $164.25B; the $108B Q3 total-revenue outlook at a 92.5% Data Center mix implies about $99.9B for Q3. Carrying roughly 18% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $118B; that makes an analyst-estimate H2 base near $218B, which SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 outperformance forecast lifts to about $261B, or roughly $426B for the year.\n8934 09-07 2 About $420B: Q1 and Q2 actual Data Center revenue total $164.25B; Q3 is approximately $100B by applying the latest 92.5% segment mix to NVIDIA’s $108B total-revenue outlook. Carrying roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives an H2 baseline near $212B; applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 outperformance estimate implies about $254B for H2 and roughly $418B for FY2027.\nGROUP linked-1 v9 71 17\n8782 07-23 4 About $348.5B: apply the roughly 90% FY2027/FY2028 Data Center share estimate to v14’s approximately $387.2B FY2027 total-revenue forecast. This is below the $350B boundary and consistent with the total-revenue dependency.\n8792 07-26 3 About $356B on the operating run-rate: $75.25B in Q1, about $83.7B for Q2 (92% of the $91B total-revenue guide), then two quarters carrying forward the approximately 11.5% sequential growth implied by that guide. The H2 outperformance view is upside evidence but lacks an absolute analyst baseline, so it does not support a defensible quantified adjustment.\n8797 07-27 3 Approximately $357B for FY2027: $75.25B reported in Q1, about $83.9B estimated for Q2 using the $91B total-revenue guide and Q1 Data Center mix, then Q3 and Q4 estimated by carrying forward the guide-implied 11.5% sequential growth.\n8884 08-18 3 About $360B at the baseline: the v14 forecast of roughly $391B total revenue multiplied by the observed 92% Data Center share. The H2 outperformance view raises the upside, but the v14 total-revenue forecast caps Data Center revenue at $391B, still below the $400B boundary.\n8890 08-19 3 About $371B: v14 forecasts roughly $403.5B of FY2027 total revenue, and applying NVIDIA’s observed 92% Data Center share gives approximately $403.5B × 92% = $371B.\n8894 08-20 3 The v14 total-revenue forecast of approximately $403.5B, applied at the observed 92% Data Center mix, implies about $371B for the segment. The quantified 20% H2 outperformance view is an upside risk, but its H2 base is unstated and applying it mechanically would imply about $413B—more than the supplied whole-company forecast.\n8905 08-23 3 About $372B: the current whole-company forecast of roughly $404B in v14 multiplied by the reported 92% Data Center share. This is a stable-mix base estimate; the new upside signals lack a quantifiable FY2027 revenue increment.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib,json,collections\nscratch=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\ndef db(a,name=\\\"views\\\"):\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{a}/prod\\\") if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else scratch/(a+\\\"-copy\\\")\n c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/(name+\\\".sqlite\\\"))+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\ndef req(a,id):\n row=db(a,\\\"archive\\\").execute(\\\"select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n return json.loads(row[0])[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"],json.loads(row[1])\nfor a,ids in [(\\\"old-1\\\",[3464,4163,4580]),(\\\"old-3\\\",[4101,4186,4222,4298,4510,5195,5369,7915,8046,8146,8311,8463,8633,8757,8969,9077,9183]),(\\\"old-2\\\",[721,853,1018,1091,4944,5598,7072]),(\\\"linked-1\\\",[8752,8756,8758,8761,8770,8838,8842])]:\n co=db(a)\n for id in ids:\n  r=co.execute(\\\"select view_id,publication_day,rung,reference,reasons,implied_outcome,cited_evidence_json from judgements where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n  print(a,id,r[\\\"view_id\\\"],r[\\\"publication_day\\\"],r[\\\"rung\\\"],r[\\\"reasons\\\"] if a in [\\\"old-1\\\",\\\"linked-1\\\"] or id in [4298,4510,5195,5369] else r[\\\"implied_outcome\\\"],sep=\\\" | \\\")\n  if a in [\\\"old-1\\\",\\\"old-2\\\",\\\"linked-1\\\"] and id in (3464,4163,4580,721,853,1018,1091,8758):\n   u,_=req(a,id)\n   ls=u.splitlines()\n   print(\\\"request notes\\\",[(l[:285],ls[i+1][:260]) for i,l in enumerate(ls) if l.startswith(\\\"- e\\\") and re.search(r\\\"\\\\\"'$20 billion|'\"\\\\\"'$396 billion|'\"\\\\\"'$5,000|4 million units|(?:trading|segment|share).{0,20}CPU|CPU.{0,20}(?:volume|shipments|segment|standalone|stand-alone|data center)|shipments.{0,60}(?:fall|million|Vera)\",l,re.I)][:20])\n'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib,json,collections\nscratch=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\ndef db(a,name=\\\"views\\\"):\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{a}/prod\\\") if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else scratch/(a+\\\"-copy\\\")\n c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/(name+\\\".sqlite\\\"))+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\ndef req(a,id):\n row=db(a,\\\"archive\\\").execute(\\\"select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n return json.loads(row[0])[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"],json.loads(row[1])\nfor a,ids in [(\\\"old-1\\\",[3464,4163,4580]),(\\\"old-3\\\",[4101,4186,4222,4298,4510,5195,5369,7915,8046,8146,8311,8463,8633,8757,8969,9077,9183]),(\\\"old-2\\\",[721,853,1018,1091,4944,5598,7072]),(\\\"linked-1\\\",[8752,8756,8758,8761,8770,8838,8842])]:\n co=db(a)\n for id in ids:\n  r=co.execute(\\\"select view_id,publication_day,rung,reference,reasons,implied_outcome,cited_evidence_json from judgements where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n  print(a,id,r[\\\"view_id\\\"],r[\\\"publication_day\\\"],r[\\\"rung\\\"],r[\\\"reasons\\\"] if a in [\\\"old-1\\\",\\\"linked-1\\\"] or id in [4298,4510,5195,5369] else r[\\\"implied_outcome\\\"],sep=\\\" | \\\")\n  if a in [\\\"old-1\\\",\\\"old-2\\\",\\\"linked-1\\\"] and id in (3464,4163,4580,721,853,1018,1091,8758):\n   u,_=req(a,id)\n   ls=u.splitlines()\n   print(\\\"request notes\\\",[(l[:285],ls[i+1][:260]) for i,l in enumerate(ls) if l.startswith(\\\"- e\\\") and re.search(r\\\"\\\\\"'$20 billion|'\"\\\\\"'$396 billion|'\"\\\\\"'$5,000|4 million units|(?:trading|segment|share).{0,20}CPU|CPU.{0,20}(?:volume|shipments|segment|standalone|stand-alone|data center)|shipments.{0,60}(?:fall|million|Vera)\",l,re.I)][:20])\n'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"old-1 | 3464 | v4 | 2026-08-14 | 1 | The market reference is roughly $392–393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 2. That is a reference, not the forecast: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance is $91 billion, and analysts expect about $103.1 billion in Q3. Carrying the strong guided growth forward, while applying the reported 20% upside expectation only to second-half data-center compute—not to total revenue—and allowing for the separately forecast $20 billion of standalone CPU sales, supports an outcome around $440 billion. That is above the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure spans products and calendar years, while SpaceX’s large capacity and spending targets are not firm FY2027 NVIDIA procurement; neither is counted dollar-for-dollar. China exclusions and competing accelerator plans temper the upside, but do not outweigh the implied uplift above the boundary.\nrequest notes [('- e65 [party_statement; fool.com analyst Adria Cimino; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] The author says NVIDIA forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.', '    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.'), ('- e544 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] NVIDIA forecast $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027 and said it was on track to dominate the stand-alone CPU market.', '    Quote: In its latest earnings report, it predicted $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year and said it was on track to dominate this market.')]\nold-1 | 4163 | v4 | 2026-08-21 | 1 | The reference is Wall Street’s roughly $392–$393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 2. The quarterly path implied by Q1 revenue, BofA’s Q2 estimate, its potential Q3 guidance, and 12% sequential growth into Q4 is about $404 billion. Allowing roughly $20 billion for forecast standalone CPU sales and only about $10 billion of incremental FY2027 SpaceX-related revenue—rather than taking the much larger, multi-period chip-spending estimate in full—puts the forecast near $434 billion. That crosses the $431.2 billion boundary into rung 1. This is an execution-sensitive forecast, not an outcome already established: the SpaceX capacity target extends to end-2027, while the OpenAI Ohio project’s first capacity is expected in 2028. China H20 sales remain a downside risk, and Morningstar’s 80% growth model is a counterweight.\nrequest notes [('- e65 [party_statement; fool.com analyst Adria Cimino; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] The author says NVIDIA forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.', '    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.'), ('- e544 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] NVIDIA forecast $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027 and said it was on track to dominate the stand-alone CPU market.', '    Quote: In its latest earnings report, it predicted $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year and said it was on track to dominate this market.')]\nold-1 | 4580 | v4 | 2026-08-25 | 2 | The market reference is about $396 billion for FY2027 (LSEG), with other Wall Street estimates around $393 billion; both sit in rung 2. A forward quarterly path of roughly $81.6 billion in Q1, $94–95 billion in Q2, $107–108 billion in Q3, and about $120 billion in Q4 implies roughly $403–405 billion before any additional contribution from standalone CPUs or SpaceX. Allowing some, but not all, of those unshipped or multi-period opportunities supports a central estimate near $410 billion. That is above the $392 billion floor but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. The roughly $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure covers multiple calendar years and products, and SpaceX’s large buildout is a year-end 2027 target, so neither can be counted as FY2027 revenue in full. Strong current Rubin production and orders support the outlook, while export and competitive risks temper the upside.\nrequest notes [('- e65 [party_statement; fool.com analyst Adria Cimino; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] The author says NVIDIA forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.', '    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.'), ('- e544 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] NVIDIA forecast $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027 and said it was on track to dominate the stand-alone CPU market.', '    Quote: In its latest earnings report, it predicted $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year and said it was on track to dominate this market.'), ('- e2478 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Morgan Stanley analyst Joseph Moore ; from gnews:067169e131df734d99650ce4 2026-08-24] Morgan Stanley analyst Joseph Moore estimated on August 5 that AMD is issuing more than $15 billion of warrants per $15 billion to $20 bi', '    Quote: Morgan Stanley analyst Joseph Moore focused on that cost in an August 5 note. He estimates AMD is effectively issuing more than $15 billion of warrants for every $15 billion to $20 billion of revenue tied to OpenAI and Meta. If the economic value of'), ('- e2577 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source LSEG ; from gnews:58fb7b4d430994417016dc48 2026-08-25] LSEG analysts forecast NVIDIA FY2027 revenue growth of 83% to $396 billion and FY2028 growth of 44%.', '    Quote: For the full year, analysts expect 83% revenue growth to $396 billion, before slowing to 44% expansion next year, according to LSEG.')]\nold-3 | 4101 | v3 | 2026-08-20 | 1 | Roughly $434–$437 billion: carry forward the prior $391–$392 billion quarterly baseline plus about $40 billion from the estimated 20% second-half Data Center compute upside, then add about $3–$4 billion for BofA’s $94–$95 billion Q2 forecast versus the $91 billion guide midpoint. The higher Q3 expectation is not added separately because it overlaps the second-half tracking uplift.\nold-3 | 4186 | v3 | 2026-08-21 | 1 | Approximately $432–436 billion: applying the roughly $40 billion implied second-half Data Center compute upside from SemiAnalysis to the roughly $392 billion annual reference, with BofA's Q2 estimate adding about $3–4 billion versus guidance, while avoiding a separate overlapping Q3 addition.\nold-3 | 4222 | v3 | 2026-08-22 | 1 | About $432–436 billion: apply SemiAnalysis's 20% upside to second-half FY2027 Data Center compute, implying roughly $40 billion above the $392 billion consensus, then include about $3–4 billion for BofA's $94–95 billion Q2 forecast versus the roughly $91 billion guide/consensus. This is a forecast, not an established result, and sits just above the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary.\nold-3 | 4298 | v3 | 2026-08-23 | 1 | The FY2027 analyst estimate is $392 billion, which is rung 2. The evidence still implies an outcome above rung 1’s $431.2 billion boundary: SemiAnalysis’s estimate of second-half FY2027 Data Center compute revenue 20% above consensus implies roughly $40 billion of upside, while BofA’s newer Q2 and following-quarter estimates are above the corresponding consensus figures. Those signals imply roughly $438–440 billion, though the lower Morningstar and sequential-growth estimates, and reported supply constraints, temper confidence. The positive China-policy theme supports the direction but is not treated as revenue evidence or assigned a separate dollar amount.\nold-3 | 4510 | v3 | 2026-08-24 | 1 | The $392 billion FY2027 analyst estimate is the reference and sits at rung 2. The reported Q1 result, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and above-consensus estimates for Q2 and Q3 support a run-rate above that reference; carrying growth forward rather than annualizing Q1 flat gives a baseline around $400 billion. The additional SemiAnalysis estimate of second-half FY2027 Data Center compute revenue 20% above consensus implies roughly $40 billion of upside, putting the forecast around $438–440 billion—above rung 1’s $431.2 billion boundary. This remains a forecast, not an established FY total: lower growth/sequential estimates and memory constraints temper confidence, but do not pull the implied outcome below the boundary. The China-authorization theme also supports upside, while the capex themes support demand.\nold-3 | 5195 | v3 | 2026-08-26 | 1 | The reference is the $396 billion FY2027 analyst estimate, in rung 2. Updating that base for Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion versus the cited $92.2 billion consensus (+$4.0 billion), and Q3 guidance of $108 billion versus the cited $103.9 billion consensus (+$4.1 billion), gives about $404 billion. Carrying forward the prior conclusion’s roughly $40 billion estimate for second-half Data Center compute outperformance implies about $444 billion, above rung 1’s $431.2 billion boundary. Supply constraints temper confidence but are not quantified enough to offset that forecast; the $40 billion uplift remains within total revenue and does not exceed the whole.\nold-3 | 5369 | v3 | 2026-08-27 | 1 | The reference is the $396 billion FY2027 analyst estimate (e2624), which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). The implied estimate is about $444 billion: carry the consensus forward, add roughly $4.0 billion for Q2's $96.2 billion result versus the $92.2 billion estimate, roughly $4.1 billion for Q3 guidance of $108 billion versus $103.9 billion consensus, and carry forward the prior conclusion's roughly $40 billion estimate for second-half Data Center compute outperformance. That is above the rung 1 boundary of $431.2 billion. This remains an aggressive forecast, not an already-proven FY outcome: Rubin shipments and purchase orders support the upside, while reported supply constraints and the ability to meet only about 70% of demand temper confidence. The implied uplift remains within total revenue and does not exceed the whole.\nold-3 | 7915 | v3 | 2026-09-11 | 1 | About $435 billion: the reported $177.837 billion first half plus $108 billion Q3 guidance and a 12% sequential Q4 carry imply about $406.8 billion before incremental upside. SemiAnalysis’s 20% above-consensus forecast is applied only to a conservatively discounted share of second-half revenue, rather than to NVIDIA’s whole revenue base, adding roughly $28 billion.\nold-3 | 8046 | v3 | 2026-09-12 | 1 | The first-half reported revenue of $177.837 billion, plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion and a 12% sequential carry into Q4, implies about $406.8 billion before the SemiAnalysis forecast of second-half data-center compute revenue 20% above consensus. Applying a discounted uplift only to that portion supports an approximate FY2027 outcome of $435 billion.\nold-3 | 8146 | v3 | 2026-09-13 | 1 | About $435 billion: $177.837 billion reported in the first half, plus the $108 billion Q3 guide and approximately $120.96 billion for Q4 using the 12% sequential carry, gives about $406.8 billion before the discounted uplift implied by SemiAnalysis's forecast that second-half data-center compute revenue will be 20% above consensus.\nold-3 | 8311 | v3 | 2026-09-14 | 1 | About $435 billion for FY2027. The reported first-half total plus the Q3 midpoint and a Q4 carry-forward of Q3's 12% sequential growth imply about $406.8 billion before applying a discount to SemiAnalysis's forecast that second-half data-center compute revenue would be 20% above consensus; the prior calculation put the resulting total near $435 billion.\nold-3 | 8463 | v3 | 2026-09-15 | 1 | About $445–447 billion: $177.837 billion reported in the first half, approximately $113–114 billion for Q3 using the specific estimate and guidance-beat evidence, and about $127 billion for Q4 if the 12% sequential growth assumption carries forward yield roughly $418 billion before the discounted uplift from the second-half Data Center compute forecast. Applying that uplift only to the relevant portion of revenue puts the total above $431.2 billion.\nold-3 | 8633 | v3 | 2026-09-16 | 1 | Approximately $445–447 billion: first-half revenue of $177.837 billion plus an estimated $113–114 billion Q3 and roughly $127–128 billion Q4 using 12% sequential growth yields about $418–420 billion. A discounted uplift for SemiAnalysis’s second-half Data Center compute projection, applied only to that revenue share, takes the implied total above $431.2 billion.\nold-3 | 8757 | v3 | 2026-09-17 | 1 | About $445–447 billion: first-half actual revenue of $177.837 billion plus an estimated $113–114 billion Q3 and roughly 12% sequential Q4 growth implies about $418–420 billion before the second-half Data Center compute uplift. Applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% projection only to that compute share lifts the forecast above $431.2 billion.\nold-3 | 8969 | v3 | 2026-09-18 | 1 | About $445–447 billion: $177.837 billion reported for the first half, plus about $113–114 billion for Q3 (e5088), then roughly 12% sequential growth for Q4, gives about $418–420 billion before the second-half data-center-compute adjustment. Applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% uplift only to that compute portion (e60), not total revenue, gives approximately $445–447 billion.\nold-3 | 9077 | v3 | 2026-09-19 | 1 | About $446 billion: $177.837 billion reported in H1, roughly $113.6 billion for Q3 using the historical beat over guidance, and about $127 billion for Q4 carrying forward 12% sequential growth, plus approximately $27–28 billion from SemiAnalysis’s 20% uplift applied only to second-half data-center compute.\nold-3 | 9183 | v3 | 2026-09-20 | 1 | About $446 billion: $177.837 billion in first-half revenue, approximately $113.6 billion for Q3 using the guidance midpoint and the cited average beat, and approximately $127 billion for Q4 by carrying forward 12% sequential growth, then adding about $27–28 billion for the estimated 20% second-half data-center-compute uplift.\nold-2 | 721 | v8 | 2026-07-14 | 3 | About $18–19 billion in FY2027: discount the $20 billion target because the evidence shows an offer to cloud customers and customer plans, but no disclosed shipment volume, deployment scale, or sales run-rate to establish the target in full.\nrequest notes [('- e51 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] NVIDIA forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.', '    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.'), ('- e211 [party_statement; TIKR author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU sales to reach $20 billion by the end of FY2027.', '    Quote: Nvidia also highlighted a new growth driver: its Vera CPU line, which the company expects to generate $20 billion in sales by the end of this fiscal year, a notable push into a market historically dominated by Intel and AMD.')]\nold-2 | 853 | v8 | 2026-07-15 | 3 | About $19.5 billion in FY2027: discount NVIDIA’s roughly $20 billion target because no shipment volume or deployment scale is shown, while allowing for the customer interest and favorable updated demand/access themes.\nrequest notes [('- e51 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] NVIDIA forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.', '    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.'), ('- e211 [party_statement; TIKR author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU sales to reach $20 billion by the end of FY2027.', '    Quote: Nvidia also highlighted a new growth driver: its Vera CPU line, which the company expects to generate $20 billion in sales by the end of this fiscal year, a notable push into a market historically dominated by Intel and AMD.'), ('- e411 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:db8d58512ec8bb97266d4b48 2026-07-15] NVIDIA estimates Vera will address a $200 billion total market and generate nearly $20 billion in CPU revenue this year.', '    Quote: Nvidia estimates that Vera will open an entirely new $200 billion total addressable market (TAM), and believes that it will generate nearly $20 billion of CPU revenue this year, positioning itself to become a major player in the server CPU market.'), ('- e429 [party_statement; fool.com author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] NVIDIA predicted $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.', '    Quote: it predicted $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year')]\nold-2 | 1018 | v8 | 2026-07-16 | 3 | About $19.5 billion in FY2027: start from the $20 billion company forecast and apply a modest execution discount because the rollout and named customer interest are not accompanied by shipment volumes, deployment scale, or a sales run-rate. This is below $20 billion but above $15 billion, so it implies rung 3.\nrequest notes [('- e51 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] NVIDIA forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.', '    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.'), ('- e211 [party_statement; TIKR author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU sales to reach $20 billion by the end of FY2027.', '    Quote: Nvidia also highlighted a new growth driver: its Vera CPU line, which the company expects to generate $20 billion in sales by the end of this fiscal year, a notable push into a market historically dominated by Intel and AMD.'), ('- e411 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:db8d58512ec8bb97266d4b48 2026-07-15] NVIDIA estimates Vera will address a $200 billion total market and generate nearly $20 billion in CPU revenue this year.', '    Quote: Nvidia estimates that Vera will open an entirely new $200 billion total addressable market (TAM), and believes that it will generate nearly $20 billion of CPU revenue this year, positioning itself to become a major player in the server CPU market.'), ('- e429 [party_statement; fool.com author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] NVIDIA predicted $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.', '    Quote: it predicted $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year')]\nold-2 | 1091 | v8 | 2026-07-17 | 3 | Approximately $19 billion in FY2027—an analytical estimate just below the company’s roughly $20 billion target, discounted because the evidence describes offers and customer intent but no quantified shipments or deployments.\nrequest notes [('- e51 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] NVIDIA forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.', '    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.'), ('- e211 [party_statement; TIKR author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU sales to reach $20 billion by the end of FY2027.', '    Quote: Nvidia also highlighted a new growth driver: its Vera CPU line, which the company expects to generate $20 billion in sales by the end of this fiscal year, a notable push into a market historically dominated by Intel and AMD.'), ('- e411 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:db8d58512ec8bb97266d4b48 2026-07-15] NVIDIA estimates Vera will address a $200 billion total market and generate nearly $20 billion in CPU revenue this year.', '    Quote: Nvidia estimates that Vera will open an entirely new $200 billion total addressable market (TAM), and believes that it will generate nearly $20 billion of CPU revenue this year, positioning itself to become a major player in the server CPU market.'), ('- e429 [party_statement; fool.com author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] NVIDIA predicted $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.', '    Quote: it predicted $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year')]\nold-2 | 4944 | v9 | 2026-08-26 | 2 | Q1 and Q2 reported Data Center revenue totals $164.25B. Applying Q2’s 92.5% Data Center share to the $108B Q3 total-revenue guide implies about $100B for Q3; carrying the roughly 12% sequential increase into Q4 gives a roughly $212B H2 baseline, and applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 outperformance forecast implies about $254B for H2 and approximately $419B for FY2027.\nold-2 | 5598 | v9 | 2026-08-29 | 2 | Approximately $422 billion: $164.25 billion in Q1–Q2, about $100 billion in Q3 using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and a roughly $115 billion Q4 estimate reflecting management's stated Q4 reacceleration. Applying SemiAnalysis's 20% second-half outperformance forecast to the roughly $215 billion baseline second half gives about $258 billion for H2 and about $422 billion for FY2027 overall.\nold-2 | 7072 | v9 | 2026-09-08 | 2 | About $414 billion: Q1 and Q2 actual Data Center revenue sum to $164.25 billion; the H2 analyst baseline of about $208 billion, derived from the carried FY2027 reference, rises to roughly $249 billion under SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 upside forecast. The $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guide and latest Data Center mix imply roughly $100 billion for Q3, consistent with—not additional to—that H2 estimate.\nlinked-1 | 8752 | v14 | 2026-07-14 | 3 | The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3; the separate description of Wall Street expectations as “well over $300 billion” is too broad to place more precisely. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that guided growth forward gives roughly $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, or about $387 billion for FY2027. That is below the $400 billion boundary and remains in rung 3. The disclosed AWS deployment plan and hyperscaler spending outlook support demand, while the HBM ramp slowdown and tighter China-related controls weigh the other way; neither supplies a quantified FY2027 revenue adjustment sufficient to cross a rung boundary. NVIDIA's $18–19 billion stand-alone CPU sales forecast is included within total revenue, not added on top, and is consistent with the whole-revenue estimate.\nlinked-1 | 8756 | v14 | 2026-07-15 | 2 | Bloomberg's $393 billion projection is the clearest market reference and sits in rung 3; the separate “well over $300 billion” expectation is too broad to locate more precisely. The Q1 result and Q2 guidance imply a roughly $387 billion FY2027 run-rate if Q2's sequential growth carries through Q3 and Q4, below the $400 billion boundary. Adding the dependency's roughly $19.5 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast, which is distinct from the existing revenue run-rate, gives about $407 billion and crosses into rung 2. This remains a forecast rather than an established result: the AWS deployment plan and $119 billion in supply-related commitments are not specified as FY2027 revenue, while HBM ramp constraints and custom-chip alternatives are downside risks.\nlinked-1 | 8758 | v9 | 2026-07-15 | 3 | The market reference is Bloomberg’s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the observed 92% Data Center mix puts the reference at about $362 billion, in rung 3. The implied Data Center forecast is about $356 billion: carrying the sequential growth from Q1 total revenue of $81.6 billion to the $91 billion Q2 guide forward gives roughly $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue, and applying the latest 92% Data Center mix gives about $356 billion. Management’s Q2 Data Center growth-acceleration comment and multiyear platform visibility add upside, but the visibility spans calendar 2026–27 and Blackwell and Rubin, so it does not establish a FY2027 Data Center amount that crosses $400 billion. Dependency v14’s roughly $407 billion total-revenue forecast includes about $19.5 billion of stand-alone CPU revenue; the approximately $356 billion Data Center estimate remains below that whole and below the rung-2 boundary.\nrequest notes []\nlinked-1 | 8761 | v9 | 2026-07-16 | 3 | The market reference is Bloomberg’s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the latest 92% Data Center mix gives about $361 billion, in rung 3. The current implied Data Center outcome is about $376 billion: carry the Q1-to-Q2 total-revenue growth implied by the $81.6 billion result and $91 billion Q2 guide through Q3 and Q4, giving roughly $387 billion for the year; apply the 92% Data Center mix (about $356 billion), then add v14’s approximately $19.5 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast as incremental Data Center revenue. This is below the $400 billion rung boundary and consistent with v14’s roughly $407 billion total-revenue forecast; the added CPU revenue raises the estimate from the prior roughly $356 billion but does not change the rung. Strong demand and the Q2 acceleration comment support the run-rate, while China restrictions, competition and supply constraints temper the upside; the broad Blackwell/Rubin visibility spans products and calendar years, so it is not taken as FY2027 Data Center revenue in full.\nlinked-1 | 8770 | v14 | 2026-07-20 | 3 | The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which is in rung 3. Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate through Q3 and Q4 implies about $387 billion for FY2027, still in rung 3 and below the $400 billion boundary. The approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add to this company-wide run-rate. Strong orders and the UAE export easing support the outlook, while the stated China exclusion and HBM supply concerns weigh against it; none provides a quantified FY2027 adjustment that moves the run-rate across a rung boundary.\nlinked-1 | 8838 | v9 | 2026-08-07 | 3 | The market reference is about $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue; applying the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate puts the segment reference near $354 billion, in rung 3. The updated total-revenue dependency implies approximately $400–410 billion for NVIDIA as a whole. Applying the roughly 90–92% Data Center share indicated by the segment mix gives about $360–377 billion; the SemiAnalysis view that second-half Data Center revenue could beat estimates by 20%, plus demand and capacity disclosures, supports a modest uplift to roughly $375–385 billion, with a central estimate around $380 billion. That remains below the $400 billion rung boundary, and the segment forecast does not exceed the whole-company forecast. Competition and China-related risks temper the upside.\nlinked-1 | 8842 | v9 | 2026-08-08 | 3 | The market reference is about $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue; applying the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate puts the segment reference near $354 billion, in rung 3. The current total-revenue forecast is about $405–415 billion. Applying a 90–92% Data Center share gives roughly $365–382 billion; Q1’s strong growth, the SemiAnalysis estimate of a 20% second-half beat versus estimates, and evidence of additional customer demand support an uplift to a central estimate around $390 billion. That remains below the $400 billion boundary, so the forecast stays in rung 3. SpaceX’s GPU commitments support demand but do not state NVIDIA revenue, and AMD competition, China-related risks, and supply constraints temper the upside.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib,json,collections\nscratch=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\ndef db(a,name=\\\"views\\\"):\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{a}/prod\\\") if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else scratch/(a+\\\"-copy\\\")\n c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/(name+\\\".sqlite\\\"))+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\ndef req(a,id):\n row=db(a,\\\"archive\\\").execute(\\\"select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n return json.loads(row[0])[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"],json.loads(row[1])\na=\\\"linked-1\\\"\nc=db(a)\nfor e,q,o,f in c.execute(\\\"select evidence_id,quote,one_line,created_from_input from evidence where lower(quote) like \\\\x27%cpu%\\\\x27 and (lower(quote) like \\\\x27%data center%\\\\x27 or lower(quote) like \\\\x27%datacenter%\\\\x27 or lower(quote) like \\\\x27%data-center%\\\\x27)\\\"):\n if re.search(r\\\"vera|grace\\\",q+\\\" \\\"+o,re.I):print(e,o,q[:540],f,sep=\\\" | \\\")\nfor id in [8752,8756,8759,8763,8766,8770]:\n u,_=req(a,id)\n for i,l in enumerate(u.splitlines()):\n  if l.startswith(\\\"- v8 \\\") or \\\" The system\\\\x27s forecast for it \\\" in l:\n   print(id,l[:420])\nprint(\\\"v8 in the linked pilot\\\",*[tuple(x) for x in c.execute(\\\"select call_id, publication_day,rung,implied_outcome from judgements where view_id=\\\\x27v8\\\\x27 and publication_day between \\\\x272026-07-14\\\\x27 and \\\\x272026-07-20\\\\x27 order by seq\\\")],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\")\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib,json,collections\nscratch=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\ndef db(a,name=\\\"views\\\"):\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{a}/prod\\\") if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else scratch/(a+\\\"-copy\\\")\n c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/(name+\\\".sqlite\\\"))+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\ndef req(a,id):\n row=db(a,\\\"archive\\\").execute(\\\"select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n return json.loads(row[0])[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"],json.loads(row[1])\na=\\\"linked-1\\\"\nc=db(a)\nfor e,q,o,f in c.execute(\\\"select evidence_id,quote,one_line,created_from_input from evidence where lower(quote) like \\\\x27%cpu%\\\\x27 and (lower(quote) like \\\\x27%data center%\\\\x27 or lower(quote) like \\\\x27%datacenter%\\\\x27 or lower(quote) like \\\\x27%data-center%\\\\x27)\\\"):\n if re.search(r\\\"vera|grace\\\",q+\\\" \\\"+o,re.I):print(e,o,q[:540],f,sep=\\\" | \\\")\nfor id in [8752,8756,8759,8763,8766,8770]:\n u,_=req(a,id)\n for i,l in enumerate(u.splitlines()):\n  if l.startswith(\\\"- v8 \\\") or \\\" The system\\\\x27s forecast for it \\\" in l:\n   print(id,l[:420])\nprint(\\\"v8 in the linked pilot\\\",*[tuple(x) for x in c.execute(\\\"select call_id, publication_day,rung,implied_outcome from judgements where view_id=\\\\x27v8\\\\x27 and publication_day between \\\\x272026-07-14\\\\x27 and \\\\x272026-07-20\\\\x27 order by seq\\\")],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\")\n'\"","aggregated_output":"e1462 | SpaceX said each Starmind satellite would include Nvidia Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs; no satellite count or deployment date was specified. | Each of the Starmind satellites will include Nvidia Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs for datacenter class space compute. | gnews:50f8d0c930476f4ae30921ed\ne2301 | SpaceXAI plans to build its future AI architecture around NVIDIA Vera Rubin and deploy Vera CPUs for agentic-AI workloads. | SpaceXAI plans to build and scale its future AI architecture around NVIDIA Vera Rubin, from data centers on Earth to orbital satellites. The company plans to deploy NVIDIA Vera CPUs to accelerate the CPU-intensive work behind agentic AI, including orchestration, tool use, code execution, data processing and simulation. | nvblog:vera-rubin-lpx-spectrum-x-nvlink-fusion\ne2496 | SpaceX said its AI1 satellites will use NVIDIA Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs, with the Vera Rubin NVL72 as the chip platform for its Starmind project. | According to a SpaceX post on X , AI1 satellites will use \"NVIDIA Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs for data center class space compute.\" SpaceX also stated that it will use NVIDIA's Vera Rubin NVL72 rackscale system as the project's chip platform. | gnews:1406ae6bc9bc60e469ddfa5d\ne3501 | NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA's estimated revenue opportunity per gigawatt rose from roughly $18 billion with Hopper to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Vera Rubin. | Our second unique capability is our full-stack AI factory platform that is expanding our share of the data center TAM [total addressable market]. Since Hopper, our revenue opportunity has grown from roughly $18 billion per gigawatt to $25 billion with Blackwell, to $40 billion with Vera Rubin, which now spans Vera CPU, Rubin GPU, NVLink, InfiniBand or Ethernet, and Groq LPU, announced earlier this week. | gnews:0f9262e1025f20b33486840c\n8752 - v8 (open; first_is_bullish) How much revenue will NVIDIA generate from stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027?: now rung 3 Neutral, judged 2026-07-14\n8752     The system's forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): About $18–19 billion in FY2027: discount the $20 billion target because the evidence shows an offer to cloud customers and customer plans, but no disclosed shipment volume, deployment scale, or sales run-rate to establish the target in full.\n8756 - v8 (open; first_is_bullish) How much revenue will NVIDIA generate from stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027?: now rung 3 Neutral, judged 2026-07-15\n8756     The system's forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): About $19.5 billion in FY2027: discount NVIDIA’s roughly $20 billion target because no shipment volume or deployment scale is shown, while allowing for the customer interest and favorable updated demand/access themes.\n8759 - v8 (open; first_is_bullish) How much revenue will NVIDIA generate from stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027?: now rung 3 Neutral, judged 2026-07-16\n8759     The system's forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): About $19.5 billion in FY2027: start from the $20 billion company forecast and apply a modest execution discount because the rollout and named customer interest are not accompanied by shipment volumes, deployment scale, or a sales run-rate. This is below $20 billion but above $15 billion, so it implies rung 3.\n8763 - v8 (open; first_is_bullish) How much revenue will NVIDIA generate from stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027?: now rung 3 Neutral, judged 2026-07-17\n8763     The system's forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): Approximately $19 billion in FY2027—an analytical estimate just below the company’s roughly $20 billion target, discounted because the evidence describes offers and customer intent but no quantified shipments or deployments.\n8766 - v8 (open; first_is_bullish) How much revenue will NVIDIA generate from stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027?: now rung 3 Neutral, judged 2026-07-17\n8766     The system's forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): Approximately $19 billion in FY2027—an analytical estimate just below the company’s roughly $20 billion target, discounted because the evidence describes offers and customer intent but no quantified shipments or deployments.\n8770 - v8 (open; first_is_bullish) How much revenue will NVIDIA generate from stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027?: now rung 3 Neutral, judged 2026-07-20\n8770     The system's forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): Approximately $19 billion in FY2027, near but below $20 billion. This discounts NVIDIA's target because shipment volume is not shown to be in hand, while the customer interest and reported cloud offering activity provide some support for approaching it.\nv8 in the linked pilot\n(721, '2026-07-14', 3, 'About $18–19 billion in FY2027: discount the $20 billion target because the evidence shows an offer to cloud customers and customer plans, but no disclosed shipment volume, deployment scale, or sales run-rate to establish the target in full.')\n(853, '2026-07-15', 3, 'About $19.5 billion in FY2027: discount NVIDIA’s roughly $20 billion target because no shipment volume or deployment scale is shown, while allowing for the customer interest and favorable updated demand/access themes.')\n(1018, '2026-07-16', 3, 'About $19.5 billion in FY2027: start from the $20 billion company forecast and apply a modest execution discount because the rollout and named customer interest are not accompanied by shipment volumes, deployment scale, or a sales run-rate. This is below $20 billion but above $15 billion, so it implies rung 3.')\n(1091, '2026-07-17', 3, 'Approximately $19 billion in FY2027—an analytical estimate just below the company’s roughly $20 billion target, discounted because the evidence describes offers and customer intent but no quantified shipments or deployments.')\n(1131, '2026-07-19', 3, \"Roughly $19 billion in FY2027: a modest discount to NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion target because the evidence does not show shipment volume or quantified customer deployments sufficient to support the full target. This falls in rung 3 ($15 billion to less than $20 billion).\")\n(1214, '2026-07-20', 3, \"Approximately $19 billion in FY2027, near but below $20 billion. This discounts NVIDIA's target because shipment volume is not shown to be in hand, while the customer interest and reported cloud offering activity provide some support for approaching it.\")\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"agent_message","text":"The gold includes Vera as a reason for a stronger second half, but it never assigns Vera a separate dollar amount. I’ll check which stored answers still lack support for the amount they add and whether the quarter-overlap problem recurs."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib,json,collections\nscratch=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\ndef db(a,name=\\\"views\\\"):\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{a}/prod\\\") if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else scratch/(a+\\\"-copy\\\")\n c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/(name+\\\".sqlite\\\"))+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\ndef req(a,id):\n row=db(a,\\\"archive\\\").execute(\\\"select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n return json.loads(row[0])[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"],json.loads(row[1])\np=db(\\\"linked-1\\\")\nfor e,q,o in p.execute(\\\"select evidence_id,quote,one_line from evidence where created_from_input in (\\\\x27truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26\\\\x27,\\\\x27nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027\\\\x27)\\\"):\n if re.search(r\\\"cpu|data.center.*revenue|compute.*networking\\\",q+\\\" \\\"+o,re.I):print(e,q,o,sep=\\\" | \\\")\nfor a,ids in [(\\\"old-3\\\",[4298,4510,5195,5369,8463]),(\\\"old-1\\\",[3464,3612,3767,3902,4037]),(\\\"old-2\\\",[4944,5473]),(\\\"control-1\\\",[8922,8925])]:\n co=db(a)\n for id in ids:\n  r=co.execute(\\\"select view_id,publication_day,rung,cited_evidence_json,implied_outcome from judgements where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n  ids=json.loads(r[\\\"cited_evidence_json\\\"])\n  print(a,id,[(e,q[:250]) for e,q in co.execute(\\\"select evidence_id,quote from evidence where evidence_id in (\\\"+\\\",\\\".join(\\\"?\\\" for _ in ids)+\\\")\\\",ids) if re.search(r\\\"semi.?analysis.{0,130}20%|20%.{0,100}(?:second.half|compute)|\\\\\"'$107|'\"\\\\\"'$108|'\"\\\\\"'$103.9|'\"\\\\\"'$113|3.1%|'\"\\\\\"'$107 billion\",q,re.I)])\n'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib,json,collections\nscratch=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\ndef db(a,name=\\\"views\\\"):\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{a}/prod\\\") if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else scratch/(a+\\\"-copy\\\")\n c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/(name+\\\".sqlite\\\"))+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\ndef req(a,id):\n row=db(a,\\\"archive\\\").execute(\\\"select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n return json.loads(row[0])[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"],json.loads(row[1])\np=db(\\\"linked-1\\\")\nfor e,q,o in p.execute(\\\"select evidence_id,quote,one_line from evidence where created_from_input in (\\\\x27truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26\\\\x27,\\\\x27nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027\\\\x27)\\\"):\n if re.search(r\\\"cpu|data.center.*revenue|compute.*networking\\\",q+\\\" \\\"+o,re.I):print(e,q,o,sep=\\\" | \\\")\nfor a,ids in [(\\\"old-3\\\",[4298,4510,5195,5369,8463]),(\\\"old-1\\\",[3464,3612,3767,3902,4037]),(\\\"old-2\\\",[4944,5473]),(\\\"control-1\\\",[8922,8925])]:\n co=db(a)\n for id in ids:\n  r=co.execute(\\\"select view_id,publication_day,rung,cited_evidence_json,implied_outcome from judgements where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n  ids=json.loads(r[\\\"cited_evidence_json\\\"])\n  print(a,id,[(e,q[:250]) for e,q in co.execute(\\\"select evidence_id,quote from evidence where evidence_id in (\\\"+\\\",\\\".join(\\\"?\\\" for _ in ids)+\\\")\\\",ids) if re.search(r\\\"semi.?analysis.{0,130}20%|20%.{0,100}(?:second.half|compute)|\\\\\"'$107|'\"\\\\\"'$108|'\"\\\\\"'$103.9|'\"\\\\\"'$113|3.1%|'\"\\\\\"'$107 billion\",q,re.I)])\n'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"e2499 | Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%. NVIDIA is not assuming any Data Center compute revenue from China in its outlook. | NVIDIA guided Q3 FY2027 revenue to $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, assuming no Data Center compute revenue from China.\ne2503 | Unveiled NVIDIA Vera , the first CPU built for AI agents, with broad adoption planned across the world’s leading technology providers. | NVIDIA unveiled its Vera CPU for AI agents and said broad adoption is planned among leading technology providers.\ne2504 | Announced that SpaceXAI will deploy NVIDIA Vera CPUs to accelerate its next generation of agentic AI applications. | SpaceXAI plans to deploy NVIDIA Vera CPUs for its next generation of agentic AI applications.\ne2533 | Q2 data center revenue increased 18% quarter-over-quarter to $89 billion with strong contributions from both sub segments hyperscale and ACIE, which includes our NeoCloud, industrial and enterprise customers. | NVIDIA Data Center segment revenue was $89 billion in Q2 FY2027, up 18% sequentially.\ne2536 | AWS is deploying an additional 2 million GPUs starting this quarter through the second quarter of fiscal '29 along with Vera, CPUs, some integrated with Rubin, other standalone. | AWS plans to deploy an additional 2 million NVIDIA GPUs beginning in Q2 FY2027 through Q2 FY2029, alongside Vera CPUs, some integrated with Rubin and some standalone.\ne2542 | Our Grace CPU introduced in 2021 has been a great success, with revenue on a trailing 12-month basis, exceeding $5 billion. | NVIDIA Grace CPU revenue exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis as of the Q2 FY2027 call.\ne2543 | We expect Vera to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, NeoCloud, AI lab, and system OEM with shipments already underway to our lead partners, including OCI, SpaceX AI and starting this quarter, AWS. | NVIDIA said Vera CPU shipments were underway to lead partners including OCI and SpaceX AI, with AWS shipments starting in Q2 FY2027, and expected deployment by every major hyperscaler, NeoCloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\ne2544 | We continue to see demand for approximately $20 billion in total server CPUs. And based on our customer demand and improving supply outlook, our preliminary expectation is for CPU revenue to more than double in fiscal '28 | NVIDIA estimated total server CPU demand at approximately $20 billion and preliminarily expected its CPU revenue to more than double in FY2028.\ne2552 | Rather than allocating their entire capacity to a single long-term offtake guarantee that lenders typically require to finance a data center independently, we have introduced a revenue-sharing structure. NVIDIA provides a take-or-pay commitment on a portion of the facility's capacity, a minimum revenue guarantee that gives lenders the confidence to underwrite the project, and in exchange, we share in a portion of the NeoCloud's revenue earned above that floor. | NVIDIA described a revenue-sharing structure in which it commits to take or pay for part of a NeoCloud facility's capacity, guarantees minimum revenue, and shares revenue above the floor.\ne2561 | In Q2, we shipped less than 1% of our total data center revenue in Hopper 200 products to customers based in China in accordance with the U.S. government licenses. | NVIDIA said Hopper 200 shipments to China-based customers were less than 1% of Q2 FY2027 Data Center revenue and were made under U.S. government licenses.\ne2562 | And given ongoing geopolitical uncertainty, there is no China data center compute revenue in our forward outlook. | NVIDIA's forward outlook included no China Data Center compute revenue.\ne2567 | We see Vera Rubin accounting for about 20% of data center revenue in Q3. | NVIDIA expected Vera Rubin to account for approximately 20% of Q3 FY2027 Data Center revenue.\nold-3 4298 [('e1116', \"And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\"), ('e2362', 'BofA expects around $94–95B of revenue , above Nvidia’s $91B guide, and sees potential next-quarter guidance of $107–108B , also above current sell-side expectations.'), ('e60', \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\")]\nold-3 4510 [('e1116', \"And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\"), ('e2362', 'BofA expects around $94–95B of revenue , above Nvidia’s $91B guide, and sees potential next-quarter guidance of $107–108B , also above current sell-side expectations.'), ('e60', \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\")]\nold-3 5195 [('e1116', \"And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\"), ('e2715', 'Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.'), ('e2862', 'For the current quarter (Q3), Nvidia expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, ahead of the average analyst projection of $103.9 billion.'), ('e60', \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\")]\nold-3 5369 [('e2715', 'Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.'), ('e60', \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\")]\nold-3 8463 [('e2715', 'Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.'), ('e4673', \"Nvidia's management sees revenue rising to $108 billion in the third quarter, and expects full-year revenue to soar 70% over last year, which is much higher than the 44% growth consensus projection from analysts.\"), ('e5088', 'However, I think Nvidia could report third-quarter revenue of around $113 billion to $114 billion.'), ('e5089', 'Over the past four quarters, actual revenue has averaged 3.1% above the high end of guidance.'), ('e60', \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\")]\nold-1 3464 [('e154', 'SemiAnalysis actually expects Nvidia’s data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 to come in 20% above Wall Street’s current expectations.')]\nold-1 3612 [('e154', 'SemiAnalysis actually expects Nvidia’s data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 to come in 20% above Wall Street’s current expectations.')]\nold-1 3767 [('e154', 'SemiAnalysis actually expects Nvidia’s data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 to come in 20% above Wall Street’s current expectations.')]\nold-1 3902 [('e154', 'SemiAnalysis actually expects Nvidia’s data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 to come in 20% above Wall Street’s current expectations.')]\nold-1 4037 [('e154', 'SemiAnalysis actually expects Nvidia’s data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 to come in 20% above Wall Street’s current expectations.'), ('e2350', 'BofA expects around $94–95B of revenue , above Nvidia’s $91B guide, and sees potential next-quarter guidance of $107–108B , also above current sell-side expectations.')]\nold-2 4944 [('e2848', 'Nvidia said it expects revenue to reach $108 billion in the third quarter, some of which will come from its next-gen Rubin GPUs.'), ('e976', \"And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\")]\nold-2 5473 [('e2848', 'Nvidia said it expects revenue to reach $108 billion in the third quarter, some of which will come from its next-gen Rubin GPUs.'), ('e976', \"And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\")]\ncontrol-1 8922 [('e2848', 'Nvidia said it expects revenue to reach $108 billion in the third quarter, some of which will come from its next-gen Rubin GPUs.'), ('e976', \"And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\")]\ncontrol-1 8925 [('e2848', 'Nvidia said it expects revenue to reach $108 billion in the third quarter, some of which will come from its next-gen Rubin GPUs.'), ('e976', \"And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\")]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -i 'double.count|overlap|already (included|counted|in|priced)|same (money|claim|figure|dollars|commitment)|avoid (adding|counting)|incremental|separatel(y|ies)|non.overlapping|one (claim|amount)' docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -i 'double.count|overlap|already (included|counted|in|priced)|same (money|claim|figure|dollars|commitment)|avoid (adding|counting)|incremental|separatel(y|ies)|non.overlapping|one (claim|amount)' docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json\"","aggregated_output":"246:   \"reasoning\": \"The big four's own plans are around $650bn for 2026 (i256, i089, i253), and NVIDIA management uses the same figure (i173). Alphabet guides up to $190bn (i232), Google Cloud's backlog nearly doubled to over $460bn (i233) and Microsoft's remaining performance obligations are $627bn (i039), so the contracted demand behind the plans is growing, and memory prices sharply higher and tight until 2028 (i252, i143) raise the dollar cost of the same build. NVIDIA reports accelerating growth as it nears $100bn quarters (i295) and SemiAnalysis sees second-half shipments above market estimates (i111), which is what the hyperscalers' cheques pay for. Against that, more than 75 US data-center projects worth about $130bn have been blocked or delayed this year (i146, i041), and Meta's CEO has spoken of slower-than-expected AI-agent progress (i292). I bet $650-720bn: in line with the plan, with the drift upward.\"\n636:    \"text\": \"NVIDIA's own projection: the big four will spend more than $1 trillion next year, up from about $650bn (i256, i089, i173, i253 all carry the same claim); no independent figure is stated.\",\n674:   \"reasoning\": \"The only 2027 figure is NVIDIA's own: the big four will spend more than $1 trillion next year, up from about $650bn (i256, i089, i173, i253 carry the same claim), a vendor's projection rather than the customers' plan. Cross-checks: the Street models 41% NVIDIA growth next year (i090, i174), which on the $650bn base is about $920bn; memory inflation with half of 2027 data-center spend going to memory (i143, i252) and Huang's AI-factory cost rising from $50-60bn to $80-100bn per gigawatt (i171) push the dollar figure up; Microsoft's $627bn (i039) and Google Cloud's $460bn (i233) backlogs support continued build. Goldman's path for all AI infrastructure, $765bn in 2026 to $1.6tn by 2031 (i161), implies only about $890bn in 2027 for a scope wider than the big four, and Meta's CEO sees slower AI-agent progress (i292). I bet $850bn-$1.05tn: the range contains NVIDIA's figure but my centre sits below it.\"\n821:    \"text\": \"SemiAnalysis reports the Kyber NVL144 rack slipped more than twelve months to 2028 because the PCB midplane cannot yet be manufactured (i100, i177, i189, i196; one claim, many outlets), that the NVL72x2 stopgap was scrapped after customer pushback (i102, i178), that the four-die Rubin Ultra was dropped for a two-die part (i179, i192, i200), that CPO-NVSwitch slips to Feynman (i180, i201), and that NVIDIA will cover the gap with more Oberon-Rubin racks (i181). NVIDIA's reply is three words, 'Our roadmap is intact' (i101), and it is unclear which roadmap is meant (i197). A 2027 product with a specific, mechanism-level delay report needs only a small slip to miss December 31, 2027. No.\",\n849:   \"reasoning\": \"NVIDIA's roadmap has Kyber and Rubin Ultra in 2027 (i277, i101). SemiAnalysis reports, with a mechanism, that the Kyber NVL144 rack slipped more than twelve months to 2028 because the PCB midplane cannot yet be manufactured (i100, i177, i189, i196; one claim carried by many outlets), that the NVL72x2 stopgap was scrapped after customer pushback (i102, i178), that the four-die Rubin Ultra was dropped for a two-die part (i179, i192, i200), that CPO-NVSwitch slips to the Feynman generation (i180, i201), and that NVIDIA will cover the gap by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks (i181). NVIDIA's reply is three words, 'Our roadmap is intact' (i101, i193, i211), and Tom's Hardware notes it is unclear whether the original or an already updated roadmap is meant (i197); suppliers' shares fell on the report (i177, i194). A 2027 product with a specific delay report needs only a small slip to miss December 31, 2027, and the question also requires the Kyber NVL144 form. I bet no, with low confidence: the material does not settle it, and the company's denial (i101, i247) is the strongest item against.\"\n860:    \"text\": \"Management's own figure: $20bn of stand-alone CPU sales this year (i095), by the end of this fiscal year (i288), nearly $20bn with no period (i080): one claim retold three times.\",\n897:   \"reasoning\": \"The reference is management's own number: $20bn of stand-alone CPU sales this year (i095), by the end of this fiscal year (i288), nearly $20bn with no period (i080), one claim retold three times. The first stand-alone CPU launches this fall (i096), so the revenue falls in roughly two quarters, and at Morgan Stanley's $5,000 per Vera CPU (i218) $20bn is four million units. Demand signals are real: Perplexity plans to deploy Vera in production after a 1.5x result on agentic coding (i262, i291), and Intel's CEO sees the CPU:GPU ratio in inference moving from 1:8 toward 1:1 (i009); but NVIDIA itself chose Intel Xeon 6 as host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8 (i008). Lean cautious on a product with no shipments yet: I bet $8-18bn, below the $20bn ambition, with the top of the range reachable if the fall ramp is steep. Management's $20bn (i288, i095) is the strongest item against.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'financ|guarantee|adds|add ' docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json | tail -28; sed -n '20,70p' docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/score_judgement.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'financ|guarantee|adds|add ' docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json | tail -28; sed -n '20,70p' docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/score_judgement.py\"","aggregated_output":"444:   \"question\": \"What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\",\n451:    \"text\": \"None stated: status quo is the disclosed pieces: Sharon AI's $4.88bn backstop (i120) and the $3.5bn of customer data-center lease guarantees added in the April quarter (i006), about $8.4bn, with the Firmus backstop announced but unsized (i122).\",\n460:    \"text\": \"Scale Firmus from Sharon: Sharon's 72MW and up to 40,000 GB300 carry $4.88bn (i120); Firmus's 360MW and up to 170,000 GPUs (i122, i004) with $25-30bn of expected customer revenue over six years (i123, i170) imply a backstop of roughly $15-25bn if the floor sits below expected revenue as SemiAnalysis describes (i117, i118). Add the lease guarantees (i006), NVIDIA's data-center lease backstops (i116), the deals SemiAnalysis says are in negotiation with announcements coming faster every quarter (i119), and a possible guarantee of OpenAI's Ohio lease on a site that could cost up to $500bn (i005). I bet $25-60bn outstanding at year-end.\",\n490:   \"reasoning\": \"Nothing in the packet totals the commitments; the disclosed pieces are Sharon AI's $4.88bn backstop on 72MW and up to 40,000 GB300s over six years (i120) and the $3.5bn of customer data-center lease guarantees added in the April quarter (i006), about $8.4bn, while the Firmus backstop is announced but unsized (i122). Firmus is five times Sharon by power and about four times by GPUs, 360MW and up to 170,000 GPUs (i122, i004), with $25-30bn of expected customer revenue over six years (i123, i170); a floor below expected revenue, as SemiAnalysis describes the structure (i117, i118), implies roughly $15-25bn. SemiAnalysis says NVIDIA has started backstopping data-center leases (i116), that more backstops exist than are public and announcements come faster every quarter (i119), and The Information says NVIDIA is negotiating to guarantee OpenAI's Ohio lease on a site that could cost up to $500bn (i005). Sharon, Firmus, the lease guarantees and a few more neoclouds put the year-end total at $25-60bn, well above what is disclosed today. The number could land lower if the 10-K reports expected rather than maximum exposure, since neither side intends to use the backstop (i118) and it covers only unused GPUs at a fixed rate (i046).\"\n509:    \"text\": \"Gross margin holds about 75% through Q2 (i281, i153) and I expect 74-76% for the year (q03). Revenue grew 85% while net income grew 211% (i302), showing operating leverage, and revenue approaches $100bn a quarter with accelerating growth (i295); a non-GAAP figure adds back stock compensation. I bet 65-69%.\",\n534:   \"reasoning\": \"No full-year figure is stated; the status quo is the Q1 operating margin of roughly 66% (i163) on a 74.9% gross margin (i084). Gross margin is guided to hold at 75% in Q2 (i281, i153) and I expect 74-76% for the year (q03). Revenue grew 85% while net income grew 211% (i302), so costs grow far slower than sales, and revenue approaches $100bn a quarter with accelerating growth (i295); a non-GAAP margin also adds back stock compensation. I bet 65-69%, in line with the Q1 level with the leverage tilt upward. The risk is the Rubin rack's 435% rise in memory content (i217) bought from a supplier guiding 86% gross margin (i142); if not passed through, both margins compress.\"\n599:    \"text\": \"NVIDIA's cash goes into other things than its own plant: $5bn into Intel (i007), stakes in Verkada and Gradium (i001, i285), $3.5bn of lease guarantees (i006), the backstop programme (i120), an $80bn buyback (i301) and a debt raise of at least $20bn (i052); the Valar Atomics AI factory is exploration only (i145). Capital spending should still grow with a company approaching $100bn quarters (i295). I bet $7-11bn.\",\n625:   \"reasoning\": \"No guidance or consensus is stated; the status quo comes from Q1 cash flow: operating cash flow $50.3bn less free cash flow $48.6bn (i131) is about $1.7bn of capital spending in the quarter, roughly $7bn a year. NVIDIA puts its cash elsewhere: $5bn into Intel (i007), stakes in Verkada and Gradium (i001, i285), $3.5bn of lease guarantees (i006), the Sharon backstop (i120), an $80bn buyback (i301) and a debt raise of at least $20bn (i052); the Valar Atomics AI factory is exploration with no schedule or investment (i145). Capital spending should still rise for a company approaching $100bn quarters (i295). I bet $7-11bn, modestly above the run-rate. The strongest counter is the run-rate itself (i131): nothing in the packet shows a step-up in NVIDIA's own plant.\"\n983:    \"text\": \"A higher FY2027 base of $400-435bn (q01: i295, i106) at the Street's 41% (i090) is $565-615bn; NVIDIA projects the big four's capex rising from $650bn to over $1 trillion in 2027 (i256, i173), a 54% step; Huang's $1 trillion of Blackwell and Rubin sales through 2027 (i079) exceeds consensus total revenue over the window; Rubin racks sell at about twice the GB300 price (i216) and Vera CPUs add a line (i288). Against: the Kyber rack may slip to 2028 (i100) with Oberon-Rubin racks filling the gap (i181), and memory stays tight until 2028 (i252). I bet $560-640bn.\",\n1013:   \"reasoning\": \"Consensus is $554bn (i037), the 41% growth analysts project for next year (i090, i174). A higher FY2027 base of $400-435bn (q01: i295, i106) at the same 41% is $565-615bn. NVIDIA projects the big four's capex rising from $650bn to over $1 trillion in 2027 (i256, i173), a 54% step, Huang's $1 trillion of Blackwell and Rubin sales through 2027 (i079) exceeds consensus total revenue over the window, Rubin racks sell at about twice the GB300 price (i216), and Vera CPUs add a line (i288). Against: SemiAnalysis has the Kyber rack slipping to 2028 with the four-die Rubin Ultra cancelled (i100, i179), which pushes the next product step out of FY2028 and leaves Oberon-Rubin racks to fill it (i181); memory stays tight until 2028 (i252); and Meta's CEO sees slower AI-agent progress (i292). I bet $560-640bn, above consensus, with low confidence eighteen months out.\"\n1017:   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a guarantee of OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease by 2027-06-30?\",\n1024:    \"text\": \"None stated: status quo is nothing signed: The Information reports NVIDIA negotiating to guarantee OpenAI's lease of a large Ohio data center (i005).\",\n1031:    \"text\": \"NVIDIA already does this: it added $3.5bn of customer data-center lease guarantees in the April quarter in exchange for share-purchase rights (i006), SemiAnalysis says it has started backstopping data-center leases (i116) with announcements coming faster every quarter (i119), and credit support is now a formal business model (i018, i002); the same source's July 1 reporting on that programme was confirmed by NVIDIA (i002). The site could cost up to $500bn if fully built (i005), so any guarantee would be partial, but the question needs only a signature. Yes, narrowly.\",\n1059:   \"reasoning\": \"The status quo is nothing signed: The Information reports NVIDIA negotiating to guarantee OpenAI's lease of a large Ohio data center (i005). NVIDIA already does this: it added $3.5bn of customer data-center lease guarantees in the April quarter in exchange for share-purchase rights (i006), SemiAnalysis says it has started backstopping data-center leases (i116) with announcements coming faster every quarter (i119), and credit support is now a formal business model (i018, i002); the same source's July 1 reporting on that programme was confirmed by NVIDIA the same day (i002). Frontier-model builders such as OpenAI are about 20% of NVIDIA's business (i286), so the relationship matters. The site could cost up to $500bn if fully built (i005), far beyond the $3.5bn and $4.88bn commitments disclosed (i006, i120), so any guarantee would be partial, but the question needs only a signature within a year. I bet yes, narrowly; the strongest item against is i005 itself: a single-source rumour with no terms.\"\n    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser()\n    ap.add_argument('run'); ap.add_argument('--day', default='2026-07-14'); ap.add_argument('--map', nargs='*', default=[])\n    args = ap.parse_args()\n    gold_path = HERE / 'gold-v1.json'\n    sha = hashlib.sha256(gold_path.read_bytes()).hexdigest()\n    gold = json.loads(gold_path.read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    if gold.get('ruler_version') != RULER_VERSION:\n        raise SystemExit(f'gold ruler_version {gold.get(\"ruler_version\")} != scorer {RULER_VERSION}: refused')\n    frozen = (HERE / 'gold-v1.sha256').read_text().split()[0] if (HERE / 'gold-v1.sha256').exists() else None\n    if GOLD_SHA256 and frozen != GOLD_SHA256:\n        raise SystemExit(f'gold-v1.sha256 {frozen} != the frozen value in this scorer {GOLD_SHA256}: refused')\n    if frozen and frozen != sha:\n        raise SystemExit(f'gold sha256 {sha} != frozen {frozen}: refused')\n    corrections = json.loads((HERE / 'corrections-v1.json').read_text()) if (HERE / 'corrections-v1.json').exists() else []\n    label = 'FROZEN' if frozen else f'DRAFT, {gold.get(\"status\")}'\n    print(f'gold v{RULER_VERSION} sha256 {sha[:16]}… [{label}]; corrections on record, not applied: {len(corrections)}; run {args.run}; window end {args.day}')\n    conn = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{args.run}/views.sqlite?mode=ro', uri=True); conn.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\n    mapping = {k: v for k, v in (m.split('=') for m in args.map)}\n    seeds = {f'q{k:02d}': f'v{k}' for k in range(1, 23)}\n    questions = {q['id']: q for q in json.loads((HERE / 'questions-v1.json').read_text(encoding='utf-8'))['questions']}\n    for qid, vid in seeds.items():     # the seeds keep their ids in every run: check the text, refuse a run that differs\n        row = conn.execute('select question from views where view_id=?', (vid,)).fetchone()\n        if not row or row['question'] != questions[qid]['question']:\n            raise SystemExit(f'{vid} in the run is not the seed question {qid}: refused')\n    mapping = {**seeds, **mapping}\n    if 'q23' not in mapping:\n        hit = conn.execute(\"select view_id from views where question like '%Ohio%' and question like '%guarantee%' order by created_seq limit 1\").fetchone()\n        if hit:\n            mapping['q23'] = hit['view_id']\n    totals = {'agree': 0, 'disagree': 0, 'no judgement': 0, 'not in run': 0, 'gold open': 0, 'no_view': 0}\n    base_lookup = [0, 0]; base_neutral = [0, 0]\n    print(f'{\"q\":<4}{\"outcome\":<14}{\"gold implied\":<28}{\"judge rung / band\":<40}{\"lookup\":<8}{\"neutral\":<8}question')\n    for entry in gold['entries']:\n        qid = entry['question_id']; q = questions[qid]\n        if entry.get('no_view'):\n            totals['no_view'] += 1; print(f'{qid:<4}{\"no_view\":<14}{\"\":<28}{\"\":<40}{\"\":<8}{\"\":<8}{q[\"question\"][:60]}'); continue\n        if not entry.get('implied_outcome'):\n            totals['gold open'] += 1; print(f'{qid:<4}{\"gold open\":<14}{\"\":<28}{\"\":<40}{\"\":<8}{\"\":<8}{q[\"question\"][:60]}'); continue\n        resolved = entry.get('resolution') or entry\n        implied, direction = resolved['implied_outcome'], resolved.get('direction')\n        vid = mapping.get(qid)\n        if not vid:\n            totals['not in run'] += 1; print(f'{qid:<4}{\"not in run\":<14}{\"\":<28}{\"\":<40}{\"\":<8}{\"\":<8}{q[\"question\"][:60]}'); continue\n        j = conn.execute('select rung from judgements where view_id=? and publication_day<=? order by judgement_id desc limit 1', (vid, args.day)).fetchone()\n        rungs = [dict(r) for r in conn.execute('select position, label, lo, hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position', (vid,))]\n        if j is None:\n            totals['no judgement'] += 1; print(f'{qid:<4}{\"no judgement\":<14}{str(implied):<28}{vid + \" never judged\":<40}{\"\":<8}{\"\":<8}{q[\"question\"][:60]}'); continue\n        chosen = rungs[j['rung'] - 1]\n        if q['unit'] == 'yes/no':\n            agree = (chosen['label'].lower() == 'yes') == bool(implied['yes_no'])\n            gold_text = 'yes' if implied['yes_no'] else 'no'; band = chosen['label']\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
