{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '421|Vera CPU|total revenue' docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7{,a}.txt | head -35; sed -n '1568,1589p;3573,3590p' docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/packet-v1.json\"","aggregated_output":"docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7.txt:4:v19 (call 1268) How much revenue will NVIDIA report from standalone Vera CPUs in FY2027?\ndocs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7.txt:8:    reasons: Rung 1 remains the best-supported forecast, but the FY2027 outcome is not established. NVIDIA's reported expectation of $20 billion in Vera CPU sales by the end of FY2027 directly supports the $20 billion threshold, while the earlier statement about nearly $20 billion in CPU sales lacks a specified period. Neither statement is fact establishing realized FY2027 revenue specifically from standalone Vera CPUs. Pricing, reported use, TAM, and analyst commentary are supportive context, but do not establish the sales volume or standalone revenue. The bullish Data Center dependency and relevant AI-infrastructure themes add demand context without resolving that standalone-revenue gap.\ndocs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7.txt:10:    reference: NVIDIA management's expectation of $20 billion in Vera CPU sales by the end of FY2027 (e260) is the applicable company reference and lies at rung 1. The nearly $20 billion CPU-sales statement (e57) does not specify a period; it does not independently set the FY2027 reference.\ndocs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7.txt:12:    reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management's FY2027 $20 billion Vera CPU sales expectation, which sits at rung 1. I discount that target because the evidence does not show shipment volumes or customer commitments sufficient to deliver it; reported Vera use establishes adoption, not scale. The explicit FY2027 target and early use still make a result above $12 billion more likely than a fall below that threshold. The bullish Data Center dependency is consistent with this estimate, and the implied standalone CPU revenue remains only a part of segment revenue.\ndocs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7.txt:20:    reasons: Rung 2 is now the best-supported judgement, but the FY2027 outcome is not established. FY2026 Data Center revenue was $193.7 billion, and FY2027 Q1 delivered $75.246 billion, up 92% year over year; Q1’s segment mix was about 92% of NVIDIA’s total revenue. Reaching $360 billion would require about $94.9 billion in each of the remaining three quarters. Management’s guidance for further Q2 growth acceleration and SemiAnalysis’s view that second-half compute revenue could exceed consensus support a path toward that threshold, although the latter is a relative forecast, not an absolute segment-revenue estimate. The total-revenue dependency’s current $392–393 billion estimate, combined with Q1’s s\ndocs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7.txt:28:v1 (call 1241) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?\ndocs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7.txt:35:    implied_outcome: About $421 billion: applying the latest guided year-over-year growth rate of nearly 95% to FY2026 revenue of more than $215.9 billion gives approximately $421 billion.\ndocs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7.txt:36:    reasons: The market reference is about $393 billion, which sits in rung 3. But Nvidia's reported Q1 growth of 85% and management's Q2 guidance for nearly 95% year-over-year growth support a faster run-rate than the reference's 82% annual growth assumption. Carrying the latest guided growth rate forward from FY2026 revenue implies about $421 billion, above the $400 billion rung boundary; this is a forecast, not an established FY2027 result.\ndocs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7.txt:37:    why_changed: The forecast moves from rung 3 to rung 2: applying the latest Q2 guided growth rate to the FY2026 revenue baseline implies about $421 billion, crossing the $400 billion boundary.\ndocs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7a.txt:4:v1 (call 1241) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?\ndocs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7a.txt:16:v19 (call 1268) How much revenue will NVIDIA report from standalone Vera CPUs in FY2027?\ndocs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7a.txt:20:    reasons: Rung 1 remains the best-supported forecast, but the FY2027 outcome is not established. NVIDIA's reported expectation of $20 billion in Vera CPU sales by the end of FY2027 directly supports the $20 billion threshold, while the earlier statement about nearly $20 billion in CPU sales lacks a specified period. Neither statement is fact establishing realized FY2027 revenue specifically from standalone Vera CPUs. Pricing, reported use, TAM, and analyst commentary are supportive context, but do not establish the sales volume or standalone revenue. The bullish Data Center dependency and relevant AI-infrastructure themes add demand context without resolving that standalone-revenue gap.\ndocs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7a.txt:22:    reference: No separate consensus figure is supplied. NVIDIA's stated expectation of $20 billion in Vera CPU sales by the end of FY2027 is the relevant company reference; it sits exactly at the rung 1 threshold.\ndocs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7a.txt:23:    implied_outcome: About $20 billion of FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue: NVIDIA's stated FY2027 sales expectation is $20 billion, and the supplied price estimate implies roughly 4 million CPUs at $5,000 each to reach it. The reported use supports some adoption, but does not establish that volume or the revenue outcome.\ndocs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7a.txt:25:    why_changed: The rung remains 1, but the key supporting fact is more specific than at the previous judgement: e260 gives a FY2027 deadline for NVIDIA's $20 billion Vera CPU sales expectation, whereas e57 was undated. Reported Vera use is also now in view, though it does not quantify revenue.\ndocs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7a.txt:56:    reasons: Rung 2 is now the best-supported judgement, but the FY2027 outcome is not established. FY2026 Data Center revenue was $193.7 billion, and FY2027 Q1 delivered $75.246 billion, up 92% year over year; Q1’s segment mix was about 92% of NVIDIA’s total revenue. Reaching $360 billion would require about $94.9 billion in each of the remaining three quarters. Management’s guidance for further Q2 growth acceleration and SemiAnalysis’s view that second-half compute revenue could exceed consensus support a path toward that threshold, although the latter is a relative forecast, not an absolute segment-revenue estimate. The total-revenue dependency’s current $392–393 billion estimate, combined with Q1’s s\ndocs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7a.txt:60:    reasons: The best forecast is about $340 billion, within rung 3. No quantified FY2027 Data Center consensus is supplied: the mechanical reference from Q1's $75.25 billion annualized is about $301 billion, which sits in rung 4, while the supplied $392–393 billion FY2027 estimate is for NVIDIA's total revenue, not Data Center. The $340 billion forecast implies roughly $88 billion per quarter over the remaining three quarters; Q2 growth acceleration and the view that second-half compute revenue will exceed consensus support that step-up, but neither supplies a dollar forecast that would justify moving above rung 3. The total-revenue dependency also constrains Data Center revenue to no more than the whole-company total. The FY2026 baseline and additional current-quarter detail strengthen the growth context but do not establish a higher rung.\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"i096\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n   \"title\": \"AMD Stock and Intel Crushed Nvidia in the First Half. Here's My Prediction for the Second Half.\",\n   \"input_id\": \"gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348\",\n   \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n   \"who\": \"NVIDIA (product schedule, as reported by the author)\",\n   \"passage\": \"it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"what\": \"first stand-alone CPU (Vera) launches as part of the Vera Rubin platform\",\n    \"period\": \"fall 2026\",\n    \"status\": \"plan\"\n   },\n   \"weight\": \"core\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"i097\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n   \"title\": \"AMD Stock and Intel Crushed Nvidia in the First Half. Here's My Prediction for the Second Half.\",\n   \"input_id\": \"gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348\",\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"i218\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why\",\n   \"input_id\": \"gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd\",\n   \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n   \"who\": \"Morgan Stanley Research\",\n   \"passage\": \"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Morgan Stanley Research (an estimate)\",\n    \"what\": \"NVIDIA's selling price per Rubin GPU and per Vera CPU\",\n    \"how_much\": \"about $55,000 per GPU; $5,000 per CPU\",\n    \"condition\": \"when sold in volume\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast\"\n   },\n   \"weight\": \"core\"\n  },\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib,json,collections\nscratch=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\ndef db(a,name=\\\"views\\\"):\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{a}/prod\\\") if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else scratch/(a+\\\"-copy\\\")\n c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/(name+\\\".sqlite\\\"))+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\ndef req(a,id):\n row=db(a,\\\"archive\\\").execute(\\\"select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n return json.loads(row[0])[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"],json.loads(row[1])\nfor a,v,lo,hi in [(\\\"control-1\\\",\\\"v14\\\",\\\"2026-07-13\\\",\\\"2026-07-20\\\"),(\\\"old-1\\\",\\\"v4\\\",\\\"2026-07-04\\\",\\\"2026-07-10\\\")]:\n for r in db(a).execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome from judgements where view_id=? and publication_day between ? and ? and call_id>? order by seq\\\",(v,lo,hi,0 if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)):\n  print(a,*tuple(r),sep=\\\" | \\\")\nprint(\\\"linked rungs\\\")\nfor v in (\\\"v8\\\",\\\"v14\\\",\\\"v9\\\"):\n print(v,[tuple(rr) for rr in db(\\\"linked-1\\\").execute(\\\"select position,lo,hi,condition from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,))])\nfor a,id,src in [(\\\"old-2\\\",853,\\\"gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd\\\"),(\\\"old-2\\\",721,\\\"gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348\\\"),(\\\"old-1\\\",4163,\\\"gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348\\\")]:\n u,_=req(a,id)\n print(a,id,\\\"has \"'$5k\",bool(re.search(r\"'\"\\\\\"'$5,000|5,000 per\",u)),\"has fall launch\",bool(re.search(r\"(?:launch|ship).{0,55}(?:fall|q3)|(?:fall|q3).{0,55}(?:launch|ship)\", \"'\"\\\\n\\\".join(l for l in u.splitlines() if re.search(r\\\"\\\\bcpu\\\\b|\\\\bvera\\\\b\\\",l,re.I)),re.I)))\n for line in u.splitlines():\n  if src in line: print(line[:480])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib,json,collections\nscratch=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\ndef db(a,name=\\\"views\\\"):\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{a}/prod\\\") if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else scratch/(a+\\\"-copy\\\")\n c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/(name+\\\".sqlite\\\"))+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\ndef req(a,id):\n row=db(a,\\\"archive\\\").execute(\\\"select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n return json.loads(row[0])[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"],json.loads(row[1])\nfor a,v,lo,hi in [(\\\"control-1\\\",\\\"v14\\\",\\\"2026-07-13\\\",\\\"2026-07-20\\\"),(\\\"old-1\\\",\\\"v4\\\",\\\"2026-07-04\\\",\\\"2026-07-10\\\")]:\n for r in db(a).execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome from judgements where view_id=? and publication_day between ? and ? and call_id>? order by seq\\\",(v,lo,hi,0 if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)):\n  print(a,*tuple(r),sep=\\\" | \\\")\nprint(\\\"linked rungs\\\")\nfor v in (\\\"v8\\\",\\\"v14\\\",\\\"v9\\\"):\n print(v,[tuple(rr) for rr in db(\\\"linked-1\\\").execute(\\\"select position,lo,hi,condition from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,))])\nfor a,id,src in [(\\\"old-2\\\",853,\\\"gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd\\\"),(\\\"old-2\\\",721,\\\"gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348\\\"),(\\\"old-1\\\",4163,\\\"gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348\\\")]:\n u,_=req(a,id)\n print(a,id,\\\"has \"'$5k\",bool(re.search(r\"'\"\\\\\"'$5,000|5,000 per\",u)),\"has fall launch\",bool(re.search(r\"(?:launch|ship).{0,55}(?:fall|q3)|(?:fall|q3).{0,55}(?:launch|ship)\", \"'\"\\\\n\\\".join(l for l in u.splitlines() if re.search(r\\\"\\\\bcpu\\\\b|\\\\bvera\\\\b\\\",l,re.I)),re.I)))\n for line in u.splitlines():\n  if src in line: print(line[:480])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"control-1 | 8744 | 2026-07-13 | 3 | Bloomberg’s FY2027 revenue projection of $393 billion is the primary market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Wall Street’s “well over $300 billion” expectation is less specific and does not locate a different rung. | About $387 billion: starting with reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the roughly 11.5% sequential increase carried forward implies approximately $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.1 billion in Q4. The resulting FY2027 total stays below $400 billion.\ncontrol-1 | 8746 | 2026-07-14 | 3 | Bloomberg’s $393 billion FY2027 revenue projection is rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). Wall Street’s “well over $300 billion” description is not specific enough to distinguish rungs. | Approximately $387 billion: start with Q1’s $81.6 billion actual and Q2’s $91 billion guidance, then carry the roughly 11.5% sequential increase into Q3 and Q4, yielding about $101.5 billion and $113.1 billion. The four-quarter total is about $387 billion, below the $400 billion boundary.\ncontrol-1 | 8748 | 2026-07-15 | 3 | Bloomberg projects $393B in FY2027 revenue, which is rung 3; the reported Wall Street expectation of “well over $300 billion” is also consistent with rung 3 but is less precise. | About $387B: carrying the Q1-to-Q2 sequential increase from $81.6B to the $91B Q2 guide forward gives roughly $101.5B in Q3 and $113.2B in Q4, for about $387B across FY2027.\ncontrol-1 | 8751 | 2026-07-16 | 2 | Bloomberg’s FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion (82% growth) is the clearest numeric market reference and sits in rung 3. The reported broader Wall Street consensus of “well over $300 billion” is less specific and does not establish a different rung. | About $417 billion for FY2027. Bloomberg’s $393 billion estimate and 82% growth imply an FY2026 base of about $216 billion; Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion at 85% growth and Q2 guidance of $91 billion at 95% growth imply about $91 billion of FY2026 revenue for those quarters, leaving roughly $125 billion for the remaining quarters. Applying the guided 95% growth rate to that remainder gives approximately $81.6B + $91B + ($125B × 1.95) = $417B.\ncontrol-1 | 8755 | 2026-07-17 | 2 | Bloomberg’s FY2027 revenue estimate of $393 billion (up 82%) is the specific market reference and sits in rung 3; the broader description “well over $300 billion” does not identify a narrower rung. | Approximately $417 billion: FY2026 revenue was $215.9 billion; Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion at 85.2% growth implies about $44.1 billion for the prior-year quarter, and the $91 billion Q2 guide at 95% growth implies about $46.7 billion. Applying 95% growth to the remaining FY2026 revenue gives about $244 billion for FY2027 Q3–Q4, for a total near $417 billion.\ncontrol-1 | 8757 | 2026-07-18 | 2 | Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales estimate is the clearest market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The supplied evidence contains no full-year FY2027 company guidance. | Approximately $417 billion: carrying the observed 85% Q1 growth and roughly 95% Q2 guided growth forward against FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about 90%–95% annual growth, with a central estimate near $417 billion.\ncontrol-1 | 8761 | 2026-07-20 | 2 | Bloomberg projects $393 billion in FY2027 sales; this reference sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). | About $417 billion for FY2027: carry the roughly 85% Q1 and 95% Q2 guided year-over-year growth run-rate forward from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, with the near-term guide and demand signals supporting a high-growth outcome. This is a forecast, not a reported FY2027 result.\nold-1 | 124 | 2026-07-04 | 2 | Wall Street's $392bn FY2027 revenue estimate; this is rung 2. | Approximately $392bn, using the stated FY2027 analyst estimate as the base case. The demand and capex signals add upside skew, but the supplied evidence does not quantify an increase to $431.2bn or above.\nold-1 | 150 | 2026-07-05 | 2 | Wall Street analysts estimate $392 billion in FY2027 revenue (e28); the $392 billion reference is rung 2, at its lower boundary. | The evidence implies $392 billion plus an upward contribution from the second-half data-center compute beat, but does not provide the dollar base or revenue mix needed to calculate that contribution. The 20% applies to one segment for half the fiscal year, so the evidence supports an outcome above the reference but does not make the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary the most likely result.\nold-1 | 286 | 2026-07-06 | 2 | Wall Street's FY2027 revenue estimate is $392 billion (e28), also described as 82% growth (e51). That is rung 2, at its $392 billion lower boundary. | Around $420 billion, in the low $400 billions: start from the $392 billion consensus reference, then allow a single-digit-percent gross uplift because SemiAnalysis's 20% upside applies only to roughly half-year second-half data-center compute revenue, a subset of total revenue. The delay/cancellation and China evidence temper that uplift but do not quantify an offset that moves the estimate below the reference.\nold-1 | 354 | 2026-07-07 | 2 | Wall Street analysts estimate $392 billion in FY2027 revenue (e28), also expressed as 82% FY2027 growth (e51). The $392 billion reference is the lower boundary of rung 2. | About $425 billion for FY2027: retain the prior roughly $420 billion central outlook supported by reported Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance, then allow a limited uplift for the $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast because the planned fall launch leaves few selling quarters and shipments are not yet shown. The resulting estimate remains below $431.2 billion.\nold-1 | 439 | 2026-07-08 | 2 | Analyst consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027 (e28, e234), within rung 2: at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion. | About $430 billion. Starting from the roughly $393 billion consensus, the reported $81.6 billion Q1 revenue and $91 billion Q2 guidance support a strong run-rate; applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% upside only to H2 data-center compute, rather than all revenue, implies an uplift that brings the total to roughly $430 billion.\nold-1 | 514 | 2026-07-09 | 2 | Analyst consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027 (e234), placing the reference in rung 2: $392 billion to below $431.2 billion. | About $430 billion: start from the roughly $393 billion reference and apply SemiAnalysis’s 20% uplift only to the estimated H2 FY2027 data-center compute portion, adding roughly $37 billion rather than applying the uplift to total revenue. The resulting estimate is below the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold.\nold-1 | 572 | 2026-07-10 | 2 | Wall Street consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027 (e234, e299), placing the reference in rung 2. | About $430 billion: start from the roughly $393 billion consensus and add about $37 billion implied by SemiAnalysis’s 20% premium on H2 FY2027 data-center compute only, not on total revenue. The reported Rubin changes and other new risks are not sized into an FY2027 revenue reduction, so the evidence does not support a precise further adjustment.\nlinked rungs\nv8 [(1, 25000000000.0, None, 'Stand-alone CPU sales exceed $25 billion in FY2027.'), (2, 20000000000.0, 25000000000.0, 'Stand-alone CPU sales are at least $20 billion and no more than $25 billion in FY2027.'), (3, 15000000000.0, 20000000000.0, 'Stand-alone CPU sales are at least $15 billion and less than $20 billion in FY2027.'), (4, 10000000000.0, 15000000000.0, 'Stand-alone CPU sales are at least $10 billion and less than $15 billion in FY2027.'), (5, None, 10000000000.0, 'Stand-alone CPU sales are less than $10 billion in FY2027.')]\nv14 [(1, 450000000000.0, None, 'Total revenue is at least $450 billion.'), (2, 400000000000.0, 450000000000.0, 'Total revenue is at least $400 billion but less than $450 billion.'), (3, 350000000000.0, 400000000000.0, 'Total revenue is at least $350 billion but less than $400 billion.'), (4, 300000000000.0, 350000000000.0, 'Total revenue is at least $300 billion but less than $350 billion.'), (5, None, 300000000000.0, 'Total revenue is less than $300 billion.')]\nv9 [(1, 450000000000.0, None, 'FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $450 billion.'), (2, 400000000000.0, 450000000000.0, 'FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $400 billion and below $450 billion.'), (3, 350000000000.0, 400000000000.0, 'FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $350 billion and below $400 billion.'), (4, 300000000000.0, 350000000000.0, 'FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $300 billion and below $350 billion.'), (5, None, 300000000000.0, 'FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is below $300 billion.')]\nold-2 853 has $5k False has fall launch False\nold-2 721 has $5k False has fall launch False\n- e48 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] The author reports that Intel holds more than 59% of total CPU market share; no measurement date is specified.\n- e49 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-05; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] The author reports that AMD's CPU market share rose from a low of about 17% in 2016 to 38% as of the item’s publication.\n- e51 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] NVIDIA forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.\nold-1 4163 has $5k False has fall launch True\n- e65 [party_statement; fool.com analyst Adria Cimino; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] The author says NVIDIA forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib,json,collections\nscratch=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\ndef db(a,name=\\\"views\\\"):\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{a}/prod\\\") if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else scratch/(a+\\\"-copy\\\")\n c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/(name+\\\".sqlite\\\"))+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\ndef req(a,id):\n row=db(a,\\\"archive\\\").execute(\\\"select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n return json.loads(row[0])[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"],json.loads(row[1])\ncn=db(\\\"old-2\\\")\nfor e,q,o,v in cn.execute(\\\"select e.evidence_id,e.quote,e.one_line,l.view_id from evidence e join evidence_links l using(evidence_id) where e.created_from_input in (\\\\x27gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348\\\\x27,\\\\x27gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd\\\\x27) order by e.created_seq,e.evidence_id\\\"):\n if re.search(r\\\"fall|5,000|xeon|vera\\\",q+\\\" \\\"+o,re.I):print(e,v,q[:260])\nco=db(\\\"linked-1\\\")\nfor id,d,i,r in co.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,implied_outcome,reasons from judgements where view_id=\\\\x27v9\\\\x27 and call_id>8737\\\"):\n if re.search(r\\\"cpu|stand.?alone|\\\\\"'$19'\"\\\\.5\\\",i+\\\" \\\"+r,re.I):\n  print(id,d,i,sep=\\\" | \\\")\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib,json,collections\nscratch=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\ndef db(a,name=\\\"views\\\"):\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{a}/prod\\\") if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else scratch/(a+\\\"-copy\\\")\n c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/(name+\\\".sqlite\\\"))+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\ndef req(a,id):\n row=db(a,\\\"archive\\\").execute(\\\"select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n return json.loads(row[0])[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"],json.loads(row[1])\ncn=db(\\\"old-2\\\")\nfor e,q,o,v in cn.execute(\\\"select e.evidence_id,e.quote,e.one_line,l.view_id from evidence e join evidence_links l using(evidence_id) where e.created_from_input in (\\\\x27gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348\\\\x27,\\\\x27gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd\\\\x27) order by e.created_seq,e.evidence_id\\\"):\n if re.search(r\\\"fall|5,000|xeon|vera\\\",q+\\\" \\\"+o,re.I):print(e,v,q[:260])\nco=db(\\\"linked-1\\\")\nfor id,d,i,r in co.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,implied_outcome,reasons from judgements where view_id=\\\\x27v9\\\\x27 and call_id>8737\\\"):\n if re.search(r\\\"cpu|stand.?alone|\\\\\"'$19'\"\\\\.5\\\",i+\\\" \\\"+r,re.I):\n  print(id,d,i,sep=\\\" | \\\")\n'\"","aggregated_output":"e50 v4 In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform.\ne67 v4 First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\n8758 | 2026-07-15 | Approximately $356 billion for FY2027 Data Center revenue: carry the sequential growth from Q1 total revenue of $81.6 billion to the $91 billion Q2 guide into Q3 and Q4, producing about $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue, then apply the observed 92% Data Center mix. This is below $400 billion and consistent with v14’s higher total forecast because its approximately $19.5 billion stand-alone CPU contribution is outside Data Center.\n8761 | 2026-07-16 | About $376 billion: carrying the $81.6 billion Q1 result and $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide forward implies roughly $387 billion for FY2027; applying the 92% Data Center mix gives about $356 billion, then adding v14’s approximately $19.5 billion stand-alone CPU forecast as incremental Data Center revenue gives about $376 billion.\n8765 | 2026-07-17 | About $375 billion: the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide versus $81.6 billion in Q1 implies roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that through Q3 and Q4 gives about $387 billion for FY2027. Applying the 92% Data Center mix gives about $356 billion, and adding v14’s approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate yields about $375 billion.\n8768 | 2026-07-18 | About $375 billion for FY2027 Data Center revenue: the Q2 $91 billion total-revenue guide carried forward at the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate implies roughly $387 billion for FY2027; applying the 92% Data Center mix gives about $356 billion, then adding v14’s approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate gives about $375 billion.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"agent_message","text":"PARTLY\n\nThe gold defeats the blanket claim that a company-wide forecast already includes every published product target. It also leaves the four linked additions unproven. Reader B uses Vera to support a stronger second half, but never adds a separate Vera dollar amount.\n\n### 1. What q01 supports\n\nReader B starts with **$172.6B for the first half** and says the **$392B consensus needs about $219B in the second half**. The reader forecasts **$228–262B** for that half, citing faster growth, compute upside, the Rubin ramp, and Vera. The Vera target is among the supporting inputs, but it is absent from the decisive-items list and gets no separate dollar allocation. D263’s own replay reached about **$421B** by carrying forward guided growth, with no separate CPU addition. A discounted Vera contribution can fit the gold’s reasoning if the evidence supports treating it as additional. The gold leaves that calculation open. [Gold q01](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:51) · [D262–D263](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:3063) · [D263 replay](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7.txt:28)\n\n- **Old-1 `354`** gives Vera limited credit because of the short selling window. That is compatible with q01. **`978`** is weaker. It adds a few billion to the prior $430B estimate, treats a target the request identifies as a repeat as new, and acknowledges “possible consensus inclusion.” **`1142` and `1208`** carry that calculation forward. The amount added is unsupported. The same dollars being present twice is unresolved.\n- **Old-1 `4163`** is the clearest departure from the gold’s reasoning. It gives the target roughly the full **$20B** without showing the volume needed to justify it. With the answer’s other assumptions held fixed, that takes **$414B to $434B**, across the $431.2B boundary. **`3464`** reads like another full allowance, though it gives no separate subtotal. **`4580`** sees the newer $396B LSEG consensus but builds from a separate quarterly forecast. The newer consensus figure alone cannot tell us how much Vera either forecast assumed.\n- **Linked-1 `8756`, `8759`, `8763`, and `8766`** add the entire **$19–19.5B forecast from v8**. The upstream judge already discounted execution risk. The downstream judge gives no basis for treating every remaining dollar as additional to the $387B forecast. The resulting $406–407B fits q01’s July 14 range, but the range cannot validate that calculation or score the later dates. [Gold q19](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:852) · [Linked requests and answers](/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/linked-1-copy/archive.sqlite)\n\n### 2. What survives H4\n\n**H4 itself is consistent with the gold.** It calls an actual double count harm. The pilot reading went further and treated four unsupported additions as confirmed double counts. I made that same inference in round 43. D711’s literal observation survives that correction. All four answers added the linked amount, and each crossed $400B against its **own** arithmetic. In the paired control, July 16–18 already reached **$417B** without the CPU addition (`8751`, `8755`, `8757`). July 15 was the only one of the four where the linked answer landed on a higher rung than the control (`8748`). I would describe the result as **four unsupported increments, with overlap unresolved**. The pilot’s registered verdict remains inconclusive. [H4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:129) · [Pilot reading](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PILOT-RESULT.md:19) · [D711](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:10660)\n\n### 3. Overlapping quarterly adjustments\n\nThe gold gives an example of combining signals in one forecast. It gives no separate Q3 beat to add on top of the second-half premium. In q02, the same compute forecast helps support the segment estimate, and **92–93% of the total** supplies the dollars. There is no second compute surcharge in the stated calculation. [Gold q02](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:101)\n\nIn old-3 v3, I found **four clear instances of stacking a Q3 uplift with the older second-half premium** (`4298`, `4510`, `5195`, `5369`) and **six related instances** where a raised Q3 estimate goes into the quarterly forecast before the older premium is applied (`8463`, `8633`, `8757`, `8969`, `9077`, `9183`). In `5195` and `5369`, taking out the entire $4.1B Q3 adjustment still leaves about $440B, on the same rung. Taking out the entire $40B second-half premium changes the rung, but that would assume all $40B overlaps. The sources do not establish the overlapping amount. `4101` and `4186` explicitly avoid stacking the Q3 adjustment. Old-2 Data Center `4944` and control-1 `8925` show a related risk of applying a July premium to a later Q3-guided forecast. This is worth reporting as a recurring calculation problem. I found no rung change attributable to the overlap alone. [Old-3 stored answers](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-3/prod/views.sqlite)\n\n### 4. What remains\n\n- **The flip is real.** `8752` keeps CPU inside the total, `8756` calls it “distinct from the existing revenue run-rate,” and `8770` puts it inside again. The $387B calculation stays essentially the same. V8’s estimate changes modestly, and none of the three answers explains why that would change what the $387B already includes. That is an unexplained change in reasoning, with no basis for forcing either answer across all dates.\n- **The upstream figure deserves its own diagnosis if taken up.** V8’s carried calls `853`, `1018`, and `1091` in old-2 supply the pilot’s $19–19.5B. The July 14 call `721` gives $18–19B, and the stored rung of $15B to below $20B overlaps q19’s $8–18B. The point estimates reveal a gap the rung can hide. The archived requests for `721` and `853` omit the two inputs Reader B used to size the discount, the fall launch and the $5,000 unit price. Even if the entire q19 range were incremental, adding it to $387B gives **$395–405B**, which straddles the boundary. It neither explains nor resolves v14’s assumption about what was already included.\n- **V9’s segment claim is unsupported and affects the forecast.** `8758` uses “outside Data Center” to justify about $356B. The next day `8761` treats the same $19.5B as Data Center revenue and gives about $376B. The rung stays the same, but the estimate moves about $20B. Q02 supports a share-based calculation, and D263’s Vera replay treats standalone CPU as part of segment revenue. The pilot request does not support the categorical exclusion in `8758`. I would record the two answers together and leave a separate design closed. [D263 Vera replay](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/proof-judging-v7.txt:4) · [Pilot answers](/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/linked-1-copy/views.sqlite)\n\n### 5. Recommendation to Alex\n\n**Close the current double-count design with nothing to build, and correct the exploratory finding in the decision record.** Keep the four additions and their effects on the forecasts in the record. Withdraw the claim that the same revenue was proved to be counted twice. Record the quarter-overlap cases separately so they can be traced to the forecasters’ assumptions if Alex takes them up. This review was read-only, with no model calls."}}
